For a permutation  $\sigma\in S_n$  let  $inv(\sigma)$  be the number of inversions. Choosing the first number of a permutation can cause anywhere from  $0$  to  $n-1$  inversions. Choosing the second can cause  $0$  to  $n-2$  inversions and so on. Hence, we get the generating function  $$ \sum_{\sigma\in S_n} x^{inv(\sigma)}=(1+x)(1+x+x^2)\cdots (1+x+x^2+...+x^{n-1}). $$  Now, let  $a_k(n)$  be the number of permutations in  $S_n$  with exactly  $k$  inversions. The above-mentioned identity implies
\begin{align*}
a_1(n)&=n-1 
a_2(n)&=a_2(n-1)+a_1(n-1) 
a_3(n)&=a_3(n-1)+a_2(n-1)+a_1(n-1).
\end{align*}
By induction we obtain
\begin{align*}
a_2(n)&=\frac{1}{2}n(n-1)-1 
a_3(n)&=\frac{1}{6}n(n^2-7).
\end{align*}

