Let  $X$  be the discrete random variable that denotes the number of checkered pairs chosen. We enumerate the bunnies, and define   $$ B_{ij}=\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
1 & \text{white rabbit }i\text{ and black rabbit }j\text{ are chosen as a pair some time in the process} 
0 & \text{otherwise} 
\end{array} 
\right. $$  where  $1\le i\le a$  and  $1\le j\le b.$  We can then decompose:  $$ X=\sum_{i=1}^{a}\sum_{j=1}^{b}B_{ij}. $$  From here, linearity of expectation tells us that  $$ \mathbb{E}[X]=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{a}\sum_{j=1}^{b}B_{ij}\right]=\sum_{i=1}^{a}\sum_{j=1}^{b}\mathbb{E}[B_{ij}]=\sum_{i=1}^{a}\sum_{j=1}^{b}\frac{1}{a+b-1}=\frac{ab}{a+b-1}. $$

