The answer is  $n=\boxed{417}$ Note that the given area condition is equivalent to  $2AC\cdot AD = AB\cdot AC + AD\cdot AE$  since  $\angle BAC=\angle CAD=\angle DAE$ . Furthermore by Ptolemy's on quadrilaterals  $ABCE$  and  $ABDE$ ,
\begin{align*}
AC\cdot 10 &= AE\cdot BC + 14\cdot CE 
AD\cdot 10 &= AE\cdot BD + 14\cdot DE
\end{align*}
For brevity, let  $BC=DE=x$ ,  $AE=y$ , and  $\angle BAC=a$ . Then  $BD=CE=2x\cos a.$  Thus rewriting our equations we get
\begin{align*}
10AC &= yx+28x\cos a 
10AD &= 2xy\cos a + 14x
\end{align*}
Multiply both sides of the equation of  $2AC\cdot AD = AB\cdot AC + AD\cdot AE$  by  $100$  to get  $$ 2\cdot 10AC\cdot 10AD=140\cdot 10AC + 10y\cdot 10AD. $$  Substituting our two above equations into this new equation yields that
\begin{align*}
2x^2(y+28\cos a)(2y\cos a +14) &= 140x(y+28\cos a) + 10yx(2y\cos a + 14) 
x(y+28\cos a)(y\cos a+7) &= 35(y+28\cos a)+5y(y\cos a + 7) 
\end{align*}
Denote  $O$  to be the center of  $\gamma$ , then on  $\triangle OBE$ , we find that  $R=\frac{5}{\sin a}$ . Since  $x=2R\sin a,$  then   $$ x=\frac{10\sin a}{\sin {3a}}=\frac{10}{4\cos^2 a -1}. $$   
Plugging this in to our equation gives that 
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{10}{4\cos^2 a -1}\right)(y+28\cos a)(y\cos a+7) &= 35(y+28\cos a)+5y(y\cos a + 7) 
2(y+28\cos a)(y\cos a+7) &= (4\cos^2 a -1)(7(y+28\cos a)+y(y\cos a + 7)) 
2(y^2\cos a + (28\cos^2 a +7)y + 196\cos a) &=(4\cos^2 a-1)(y^2\cos a+14y+196\cos a) 
2y^2\cos a + (56\cos^2 a +14)y + 392\cos a &= y^2(\cos a)(4\cos^2 a-1)+14y(4\cos^2 a -1)+196\cos a(4\cos^2 a -1)
0 &= y^2(4\cos^3 a -3\cos a)-28y+196(4\cos^3 a-3\cos a)
0 &= y^2\cos (3a) -28y + 196\cos (3a)
\end{align*}
Whew. That simplified nicely. This gives us that   $$ y=AE=\frac{14\pm 14\sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}. $$  **<span style="color:#f00">Case 1:</span>**  $AE=\frac{14+ 14\sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}$ We now apply LoC on  $\triangle BAE$ . This gives us that
\begin{align*}
14^2+14^2\left(\frac{1+ \sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}\right)^2-2\cdot 14^2\cdot \left(\frac{1+ \sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}\right)\cos(3a) &=100. 
1+\frac{1+ 2\sin(3a)+\sin^2 (3a)}{1-\sin^2 (3a)}-2(1+\sin(3a)) &=\frac{25}{49} 
98\sin^2 (3a) + 98\sin^3 (3a)&=25-25\sin^2 (3a) 
98\sin^3 (3a) + 123\sin^2 (3a)-25 &= 0
(\sin(3a) +1)(98\sin^2 (3a) +25\sin (3a)-25) &=0
\end{align*}
Clearly since  $\angle BAE < 180^{\circ}$ , then we have   $$ \sin(3a)=\frac{\sqrt{10425}-25}{196} $$  **<span style="color:#f00">Case 2:</span>**  $AE=\frac{14- 14\sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}$ Pretty much the same as case one, with some changes to signs. (We could also assume that the other sine value must be the conjugate of case one due to the answer format but I've added this case just for sake of completeness and rigor)

We now apply LoC on  $\triangle BAE$ . This gives us that
\begin{align*}
14^2+14^2\left(\frac{1- \sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}\right)^2-2\cdot 14^2\cdot \left(\frac{1- \sin(3a)}{\cos(3a)}\right)\cos(3a) &=100. 
1+\frac{1- 2\sin(3a)+\sin^2 (3a)}{1-\sin^2 (3a)}-2(1-\sin(3a)) &=\frac{25}{49} 
98\sin^2 (3a) - 98\sin^3 (3a)&=25-25\sin^2 (3a) 
98\sin^3 (3a) - 123\sin^2 (3a)+25 &= 0
(\sin(3a) -1)(98\sin^2 (3a) -25\sin (3a)-25) &=0
\end{align*}
Clearly since  $\angle BAE < 180^{\circ}$ , then we have   $$ \sin(3a)=\frac{\sqrt{10425}+25}{196} $$  Finally, the sum of the two radii by extended LoS is   $$ 5\left(\frac{196}{\sqrt{10425}-25}+\frac{196}{\sqrt{10425}+25}\right)=\frac{5\cdot2\cdot196\sqrt{10425}}{9800}=\sqrt{\boxed{417}} $$

Here's a solution without LTE. 

It's clear that  $m, n$  divide  $10!$ . Let  $d = gcd(m, n)$  Taking things modulo  $2^n  - 1$ , observe that 
\begin{align*}
\frac{2^{10!} - 1}{2^m - 1} &= 2^{10! - m} + 2^{10! - 2m} + \ldots  + 2^m + 2^0 
&\equiv \frac{10!}{m} \cdot \frac{d}{n} (2^{n - d} + 2^{n - 2d} + \ldots + 2^d + 1) 
&\equiv \frac{10!d}{mn}\cdot \frac{2^n - 1}{2^d - 1}
\end{align*}
 by CRT, so  $2^n - 1$  divides  $\frac{2^{10!} - 1}{2^m - 1} \iff 2^d - 1$  divides  $\frac{10!d}{mn} \implies 2^d - 1$  divides  $10!$ . Then it's easily checked that  $d \in \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6\}$  courtesy of Zsigmondy and routine calculations give  $5509$ .

