We complementary count.

Note that for  $\left \lceil \frac{n}{2^p} \right \rceil$  to be a multiple of 5, then  $n$  must be of the form   $$ n=k\cdot (5\cdot 2^p) -r \quad \quad 0\leq r<2^p. $$  This motivates us to consider all  $n$  modulo  $5, 5\cdot 2, 5\cdot 2^2,\dots 5\cdot 2^7$  since for  $2^8$  and above there are no possible  $1\leq n\leq 1024.$  Alright, we now proceed with casework on  $p$  such that  $5\mid \left \lceil \frac{n}{2^p}\right\rceil$  but  $5\nmid \left \lceil \frac{n}{2^q}\right\rceil$  for  $q<p.$  Denote such value as  $p_0$ .**Case 1:**  $p_0=0$ .
Note that there can only be one such  $r$  such that this is true:  $r=0.$  Thus,  $n\equiv 0\pmod 5$ , which has  $\boxed{204}$  possible values.**Case 2:**  $p_0=1.$  
Normally, we would have  $r\in\{0,1\},$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {10},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then  $r\neq0$  otherwise that would reduce to case 1. Hence, there is only  $n\equiv -1\pmod {10}$  which has  $\boxed{102}$  values.**Case 3:**  $p_0=2.$ Normally, we would have  $r\in\{0,1,\dots, 3\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {20},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Hence,  $r\neq 0$  (case 1) and  $r\neq 1$  (case 2). Hence,  $n\equiv -2,-3\pmod {20}$  which has  $\boxed{102}$  values for  $n$ .**Case 4:**  $p_0=3.$ Normally  $r\in\{0,1,\dots,7\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {40},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Hence, we can only have  $r\equiv 4,6,7$ . Thus,  $n\equiv -4,-6,-7\pmod{40},$  which has  $\boxed{75}$  values for  $n$ .**Case 5:**  $p_0=4.$ Normally  $r\in\{0,1,\dots,15\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {80},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Hence, we can only have  $r\equiv 8,9,12,13,14$ . Thus,  $n\equiv -8,-9,-12,-13,-14\pmod{80},$  which has  $\boxed{60}$  values for  $n$ .**Case 6:**  $p_0=5.$ Normally  $r\in\{0,1,\dots,31\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {160},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Hence, we can only have  $r\equiv 16,17,18,19,24,26,27,28,29$ . Thus,  $n\equiv -16,-17,-18,-19,-24,-26,-27,-28,-29\pmod{160},$  which has  $\boxed{54}$  values for  $n$ .

*Remark: Note that to find the number of residues, you just take each residue from a previous case and add its modulus onto that residue and if the resulting sum is in the new case, then that cannot be a residue. I'm only listing out the residues for brevity and rigor. For instance, in case 6, we know that we cannot have residues 0,5,10,15,20,25,30 because of case 1, we cannot have residues 1,11,21,31 because of case 2, and so on. We will be using this for case 8.***Case 7:**  $p_0=6.$ Normally  $r\in\{0,1,\dots,63\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {320},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Hence, we can only have  $r\equiv 32, 33,34,36,37,38,39,48,49,52,53,54,56,57,58,59$ . Thus,  $n\equiv -32, -33,-34,-36,-37,-38,-39,-48,-49,-52,-53,-54,-56,-57,-58,-59\pmod{320},$  which has  $\boxed{48}$  values for  $n$ .**Case 8:**  $p_0=7.$ Normally  $r\in\{0,1,\dots,127\}$  and  $n\equiv -r\pmod {640},$  but since  $p_0$  is minimal, then we cannot have values of  $r$  that reduce to any previous case. Okay. Since there are a lot of residues for this one, we will use the method mentioned in the remark to find the appropriate residues. We will find which residues we cannot have:
\begin{align*}
\text{From case 1}&: r\neq 0,5,10,15,\dots, 125 \implies \boxed{26} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 2}&: r\neq 1,11,21,31,\dots, 121 \implies \boxed{13} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 3}&: r\neq 2,3,22,23,\dots, 122,123 \implies \boxed{14} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 4}&: r\neq 4,6,7,44,46,47\dots, 124,126,127 \implies \boxed{12} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 5}&: r\neq 8,9,12,13,14,88,89,92,93,94 \implies \boxed{10} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 6}&: r\neq 16,17,18,19,24,26,27,28,29 \implies \boxed{9} \quad\text{bad residues}
\text{From case 7}&: r\neq 32, 33,34,36,37,38,39,48,49,52,53,54,56,57,58,59 \implies \boxed{16} \quad\text{bad residues}
\end{align*}
Hence, there are in total  $100$  bad residues, so there are  $128-100=28$  residues and hence  $\boxed{28}$  values of  $n$  for case 8.

Thus our complementary count gave  $204+102+102+75+60+54+48+28=673,$  so there are  $1024-673=\boxed{351}$  values of  $n$  such that there are no multiples of  $5$

candidates:
Let  $a_k$  be the number of integers  $0<n\le2^k$  where  $k\ge0$  such that the sequence  $\lceil n \rceil, \lceil n/2 \rceil, \lceil n/4 \rceil, \ldots$  does not contain any multiple of 5.**Important:** When calculating  $a_k,$  we only need to focus on  $\lceil n \rceil, \lceil n/2 \rceil, \lceil n/4 \rceil, \ldots , \lceil n/2^{k-3} \rceil$  because for  $i\ge k-2,$    $$ 0<\lceil n/2^i \rceil \le \lceil 2^k/2^{k-2} \rceil = 2^2=4, $$   which obviously cannot be  $0\pmod5.$ The first few values of  $a_k$  are\begin{tabular}{c|ccccccccc}
k&0&1&2&3&4&5&6&7& $\cdots$  \hline $a_k$ &1&2&4&7&12&21&37&65& $\cdots$ \end{tabular}
The key claim is  $a_k=a_{k-1}+a_{k-2}+a_{k-4}.$ -----
*Proof.* To calculate  $a_k$  we need to find the number of integers  $0<n\le2^k$  so that  $\lceil n \rceil, \lceil n/2 \rceil, \lceil n/4 \rceil, \ldots \not\equiv 0\pmod{5}.$ **Subcase 1:**  $0<n\le2^{k-1}$ This is just  $a_{k-1}.$ **Subcase 2:**  $2^{k-1}=4\cdot2^{k-3}<n\le5\cdot2^{k-3}$  $0,$  all of these numbers have  $\lceil n/2^{k-3} \rceil = 5.$ **Subcase 3:**  $5\cdot2^{k-3}=10\cdot2^{k-4} < n \le 14\cdot2^{k-4}$ There are  $2^{k-2}$  numbers here and they correspond to  $a_{k-2}.$  This is because for every number  $j$  in the set,  $$ j\equiv j-5\cdot2^{k-3} \pmod{5\cdot2^a} $$  for  $0\le a\le k-3.$ **Subcase 4:**  $14\cdot 2^{k-4}<n\le 15\cdot2^{k-4}$  $0$  cases, they all have  $\lceil n/2^{k-4} \rceil = 15.$ **Subcase 5:**  $15\cdot2^{k-4} < n \le 2^k$ There are  $2^{k-4}$  numbers here and they correspond to  $a_{k-4}.$  This is because for every number  $j$  in the set,  $$ j\equiv j-15\cdot2^{k-4} \pmod{5\cdot2^a} $$  for  $0\le a\le k-4.$  We do not need to worry about  $a=k-3$  because the largest multiple of  $5\cdot2^{k-3}$  under  $2^k$  is  $5\cdot2^{k-3},$  and all  $n$  in this range satisfy  $n>5\cdot2^{k-3}.$ -----
Now, we get  $a_8 = 114, a_9 = 200, \boxed{a_{10}=351.}$

