First, note that  $\angle APE = 180^\circ - \angle APB = 60^\circ$  and  $\angle APF = 180^\circ - \angle APC = 60^\circ$ , so  $AEPF$  is cyclic with  $AE = AF$ . It follows by Miquel Point that  $Q \in \overline{BC}$ . 

Now we will use the following lemma: **Lemma.** Let  $ABC$  be a triangle with  $E \in \overline{AC}$ ,  $F \in \overline{AB}$ , and  $P = \overline{BE} \cap \overline{CF}$ . If  $AEPF$  is cyclic, then  $BF \cdot BA + CE \cdot CA = BC^2$ . 

<details><summary>Proof</summary>Again,  $AEPF$  cyclic is equivalent to  $(BFP)$  and  $(CEQ)$  intersect at some point  $Q \in \overline{BC}$ . Angle chase (or radical axis) gives  $AFQC$ ,  $AEQB$  cyclic. Then PoP gives   $$ BQ \cdot BC = BF \cdot BA $$   and   $$ CQ \cdot CB = CE \cdot CA. $$   We are done by adding these equations.</details>

Applying this lemma to our problem with  $AE = AF = x$  gives   $$ (5-x)(5)+(8-x)(8) = 7^2 \implies x = \frac{40}{13}. $$   Note that  $\angle FQB = \angle FPB = 60^\circ$  and  $\angle CQE = \angle EPC = 60^\circ$ , which means   $$ QE + QF = 2(QZ + QY) = 2(7-BZ-CY) = 2(7 - BF \cos B - CE \cos C). $$   Routine calculations gives an answer of  $40/7$ , or  $\boxed{4007}$ . **Remark**:  $Q$  is the point on  $\overline{BC}$  such that  $QE + QF$  is minimized.

