Solved with **rama1728.****Claim:** If for two odd primes  $p < q$  we have  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2) = \operatorname{ord}_p(2)$  then  $(p,2)$  is strange.**Proof:** If  $F(n)F(n+1)=2p,$  then either  $(n,n+1)$  is either  $(2^k, 2^k+1)$  or  $(2^k-1, 2^k)$  for some positive integer  $k.$  In the latter case, note  $p \mid 2^{k}-1 \implies \operatorname{ord}_p(2) \mid k \implies \operatorname{ord}_q(2) \mid k \implies q \mid 2^k-1.$  In the former case,  $p \mid 2^{k}+1 \implies \operatorname{ord}_p(2) =\operatorname{ord}_q(2) = 2k \implies q \mid 2^{2k}-1,$  and since  $q \nmid 2^k-1$  because  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2) \ne k,$  we have  $q \mid 2^k+1.$   $\square$ It suffices to show there are infinitely many distinct pairs of odd primes  $p,q$  where  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2) =  \operatorname{ord}_p(2).$  Take any prime  $P = 2k+1 > 5$  (obviously infinitely many) and note
\begin{align*}
2^{2P} + 1 &=4\cdot 2^{4k} + 1
&=(2^{2k+1}+2^{k+1}+1)(2^{2k+1}-2^{k+1}+1).
\end{align*}
Note  $\gcd(2^{2k+1}+2^{k+1}+1, 2^{2k+1}-2^{k+1}+1)=\gcd(2^{k+2}, 2^{2k+1}-2^{k+1}+1)=1,$   $3 \nmid 2^{2P}+1,$  and  $25 \nmid 2^{2P}+1$  since  $10 \nmid 2P.$  So we can take two distinct primes  $p,q > 5$  dividing  $2^{2P}+1,$  so  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2), \operatorname{ord}_p(2) \mid 4P.$   Obviously now  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2), \operatorname{ord}_p(2) > 4,$  so  $\operatorname{ord}_q(2) = \operatorname{ord}_p(2) = 4P$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$

We start with the following preliminary observation.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**All  $a-$ good integers  $b$  must be even.</span>

*Proof :*To see why, consider  $n>1$  such that  $an+1$  is prime (which exists due to Dirichlet's theorem on arithmetic progressions). Now, if  $b$  is  $a-$ good then  $\binom{an}{b}\equiv 1 \pmod{an+1}$  and thus,
\begin{align*}
(an)! & \equiv b!(an-b)! \pmod{an+1}
-1 & \equiv \frac{-b!}{(an-b+1)\dots (an)} \pmod{an+1}
-1 & \equiv (-1)^{1-b} \pmod{an+1}
\end{align*}
by Wilson's Theorem (and since  $an-b+1<\dots < an < an+1$ ). Thus,  $1-b$  is odd which implies the claim.


Note that if  $\gcd((b+1)(b+2),an+1)=1$  for all positive integers  $n$  where  $b$  is an  $a-$ good positive integer,
\[\binom{an}{b+2}=\binom{an}{b} \cdot \frac{(an-b-1)(an-b)}{(b+1)(b+2)} \equiv \binom{an}{b} \cdot \frac{(-b-2)(-b-1)}{(b+1)(b+2)}\equiv 1 \pmod{an+1}\]
for all positive integers  $n$  which implies that  $b+2$  is also  $a-$ good. Hence, if  $b$  is  $a-$ good but  $b+2$  is not, we must have some prime  $p$  and positive integer  $n_0$  such that  $p \mid (b+1)(b+2)$  and  $p \mid an_0+1$ .

But note that if  $b+1$  is not prime,  $p<b+1$  as  $b+2$  is even and hence not prime. Thus,  $p \mid b!$ . Hence, there must exist a prime  $p$  which is a factor of  $b!$  for which there exists some positive integer  $n_0$  such that  $p \mid an_0+1$ . However then, for any positive integer  $n$ , 
\[\binom{an}{b}=\frac{(an)(an-1)\dots (an-b+1)}{b!}\equiv 1 \pmod{p}\]
Since we know  $p \mid an+1$  it is clear that  $\gcd(a,p)=1$  and hence, we may consider  $n \equiv \frac{p-1}{a} \pmod{p^{\nu_p(b!)+1}}$  so that  $p^{\nu_p(b!)+1} \mid an-p+1$ . Hence,  $\nu_p((an)(an-1)\dots (an-b+1))>\nu_p(b!)$  which implies that  $p \mid \binom{an}{b}$  for this choice of  $n$  which is a clear contradiction to our assumption that  $b$  is  $a-$ good. Hence, it is impossible for such a prime to exist and it follows that  $b+1$  is prime as desired.

