\node at (2.5,0) { $0$ }; \node at (-2.5,0) { $0$ }; \node at (0,3.5) { $+1$ }; \node at (0,-3.5) { $-1$ };
\draw[fill = magenta, rotate = 270] (10pt,0pt)--(-10pt,6pt)--(-6pt,0pt)--(-10pt,-6pt)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture} ");[/asy] $\textbf{\text{Proof.}}$  Since each vertex has degree  $3$ , only four faces can be affected by this modification. Moreover, the two adjacent faces are also unaffected, as they got both an increase of  $1$  and a decrease of  $1$ . We are done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\color{green} \rule{25cm}{1pt}$  $\color{green} \textbf{\text{Finishing: Existing even cycle.}}$  Let  $v_1v_2\ldots v_{2k}$  be an even cycle. So, however we toggle/modify the values of edges  $v_iv_{i+1}$ , the only affected faces are the central faces and the faces adjacent to it. Toggling  $v_1/v_2$ ,  $v_3/v_4$ ,  $\ldots$ ,  $v_{2k-1}/v_{2k}$  (a.k.a. adding  $1$  and substracting  $1$  according to the  $\textbf{\text{First Lemma}}$ ) yields the construction. 
