\[ \dfrac{|V|}{2} = \dfrac{|E|}{3} \]
we can set  $|V|=2k$  and  $|E| = 3k$  for some  $k$ . It follows that  $|F| = k+2$  by  $V-E+F=2$ . 
If all faces have odd edges, then the total number of incidences  $(\text{edge},\text{face})$ [list = disc] [*] equals to  $3k$  additions of odd numbers, by viewing through summation of edges in  $F_1,F_2,\ldots,F_{3k}$  (each of which is odd), but [*] must be even, since each edge is incident to two faces. [/list]

So,  $k$  is even, and so we can represent  $(V,E,F)$  as  $(4l, 6l, 2l+2)$ , with  $l \geq 2$  (i.e. as  $l \ne 1$  by the problem condition.) 
Now create an  $``\text{auxiliary graph}" A_G $  which 
[list = disc] [*] vertices consist of the  $2l+2$  faces, and [*] edges connecting two vertices (i.e. faces) if and only if there exists some edge in the original graph, that when toggled as in the  $\textbf{\text{First Lemma}}$ , the two faces are the unaffected ones. [/list]
In total, there exists  $6l$  edges in  $G$  (and also in  $A_G$ ), meaning that a cycle must exist in  $A_G$ , since (of course) there are only  $2l+2$  vertices in  $A_G$ . When a cycle forms in  $A_G$ , we can finish by toggling the matching edges of the cycle of  $A_G$  in  $G$  --- this ensures that each face gets a  $``+1"$  and a  $``-1"$ . It might seem that we are done, but there's a catch.  $\color{blue} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$  $\color{blue} \textbf{\text{Ensuring no cycle subgraphs.}}$  However, this simple and  $``\text{suspiciously weak}"$  construction cannot ensure that the toggled edges will result in not all  $G$ 's vertices being non-zero. Observing a bit more closely, this can happen if there exists a cycle in the  $6l$  edges we pick (which of course there is.) 
In fact, we're going to do much better by only selecting some  $4l-1 \geq 2l+2$  distinguished edges. 
Pick a connected component  $C$  in  $G$ , and reiterate the working in  $\color{green} \textbf{\text{Finishing: Remaining Cases}}$ . Now since  $C$  has some  $4c$  vertices, pick a spanning tree in  $C$  with  $4c-1$  edges. Transform this into  $A_C$  to find a cycle in  $A_C$  and back --- just basically repeating the same process with a bit less edges. Since a tree in  $C$  cannot have a cycle subgraph in  $C$ , we are finally done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$  

