For the first time in a while, after considering cycles or union of cycles, a new idea popped up: using the IMO 2020-3 statement (spoiler alert!) here: to partition a 3-regular graph into a 1-regular and a collection of Hamiltonian cycles. However, this took up most of my time, as I was unable to prove it. Looking it up [url = https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/1574260/every-bridgless-planar-3-regular-graph-is-3-edge-colorable ] online [/url] later, I found out that this isn't easy (four colour theorem!) After all, what use is a labeling, when you can't get the problem statement to behave nicely with respect to that labeling? 
