Seeing that perturbing four vertices only affects two faces is statistically improbable, I wondered how perturbing only two vertices --- adjacent vertices, in the best case --- would affect the configuration as a whole. I finally got the whole configuration only by  $\textbf{altering six vertices!}$  Adding to the bonus, there was a cycle going on between three faces, and seeing that there are  $6l$  edges and only  $2l+2$  faces brought upon the idea to build an entirely new auxiliary graph. 
As I was trying to write up the solution, I again realized that I hadn't tackled the  $\textbf{tetrahedron problem}$  at all, because, technically, I could apply this procedure to a tetrahedron with no obstructions. Sure, there are 6 edges and 6 faces, but what's wrong with that? Then, I noticed the inevitable doom that however one tries to form a cycle with the faces, one would eventually have a cycle with the edges. 
From all the previous working, induction hasn't been helpful --- so I placed my hopes on the only thing left, which is considering the size of  $|E_G|$ , which is small in a tetrahedron. In large graphs, I had more free choices because of the  $6l$  available edges; I would be instantly free if from those edges, I can guarantee that with  $2l+2$  hand-picked edges, there's no cycle subgraph in  $G$ . How hard is it, when the graph has double the amount of vertices as our goal? It's easy when some  $2l$  edges are already picked to be disjoint, and I flashed back to the IMO 2020-3 problem. 
\[\begin{tabular}{p{12cm}} $\rule{4cm}{0.5pt} \hspace{1cm} \bigstar \hspace{1cm} \rule{4cm}{0.5pt}$  
\end{tabular}  \]
If the  $6l$  edges are easily partitioned into 3 groups, with each being 1-regular, I can actually just add 2 random edges and as long as no triangle or 4-cycle is formed, then I'm free. However, the goal isn't necessarily partitioning  $G$  into 3 groups, but to ensure that no cycle subgraph is formed within the edge of our picking. Well, and I realized that with  $4l$  vertices, then there must always exist a large collection of edges with no cycle subgraph --- a spanning tree! 
A final problem occurs: what if  $G$  is not connected and our  $``\text{diagram}"$  is a lie? What if  $G$  actually consists of arbitrary forests? This question got me stumped in the 2020 IMO problem, and here I can just pick a connected component of  $G$  and be done!
<details><summary>Spoilers for 2020-3 </summary>When looking for cycles in that problem, one had to consider an Eulerian circuit or a collection of some; and it's impossible to scan for Eulerian circuits in another component, well, if we're only looking at edges in a particular component! The (first) official solution nicely partitions  $G$  into connected components, and here it's also applied in my Solution.</details>
