Introduce the Orthic triangle  $H_AH_BH_C$  of  $\triangle ABC$ .

Firstly we will exploit the condition (Angle Bisector of  $\angle BHC$ ). Let  $HY$  be the Bisector of  $\angle BHC$  where  $Y\in BC$ . 
Now drop a Perpendicular from  $A$  to  $KH$ . Let  $AX\perp KH$  where  $X\in KH$ . Now notice that  $\angle AXK=\angle AQK=90^\circ\implies X\in\odot(AH)$  and similarly  $\angle AXH=\angle AH_BH\implies X\in\odot(AH)$ . So,  $\odot(AK)\cap\odot(AH)=\{A,X\}$ . Let  $AX\cap QH=J$ . **<span style="color:#f00">Claim 1:-</span>**.   $AP=AQ$ <details><summary>Proof</summary>$$ \angle HYB=180^\circ-\angle HBY-\angle BHY=180^\circ-(90^\circ-\angle C)-(90^\circ-\frac{\angle A}{2})=\angle C+\frac{\angle A}{2} $$   Hence we get that    $$ \angle BPY=\angle APQ=180^\circ-\angle HYB-\angle B=\frac{\angle  A}{2} $$   now  $\angle AQP=\angle A-\angle APQ=\frac{\angle A}{2}=\angle APQ\implies AP=AQ$ .</details>**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 2:-</span>**.   $(Q,P;J,H)$  is Harmonic.
<details><summary>Proof</summary>Notice that  $QXPA$  is a cyclic quadrilateral and from **Claim 1** we get that  $AP=AQ$ . Hence,  $\angle QXJ=\angle JXP$  also  $\angle JXH=90^\circ\implies (Q,P;J,H)$  is Harmonic.</details>**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 3:-</span>**  $AX,H_BH_C,BC$  are concurrent.
<details><summary>Proof</summary>Let  $AX\cap BC=X_A'$ . Now we will throw a perspective from  $A$  to this Harmonic Bundle...  $$ -1=(Q,P;J,H)\overset{A}{=}(C,B;X_A',H_A) $$  Let  $H_BH_C\cap BC=X_A$ . So,  $(X_A,H_A;B,C)$  is also Harmonic. Hence,  $X_A'\equiv X_A$ . So,  $AX,H_BH_C,BC$  concurs at the  $X_A$ .</details>**<span style="color:#f00">Claim 4:-</span>**  $X\in\odot(ABC)$ .
<details><summary>Proof</summary>Note that  $A,X,H_C,H_B$  and  $B,H_B,H_C,C$  are concyclic. Hence,   $$ X_AX.XA=X_AH_C.X_AH_B=X_AB.X_AC\implies X\in\odot(ABC) $$  .</details>

Now let  $XH\cap \odot(ABC)=A'$ . So as  $\angle AXA'=90^\circ$ ,  $A'$  is the  $A-\text{ antipode}$  of  $\odot(ABC)$ . So, now notice that  $CH\|BA'$  and  $BH\|CA'\implies BHCA'$  is a parallelogram. Now in a parallelogram diagonals bisect each other so  $HA'$  bisects  $BC$  , hence  $KH$  bisects  $BC$ .  $\blacksquare$

