The set  $S_1 \cup S_2$  contains any given element with probability  $\frac{3}{4}$  independently of any other element. Let  $\omega$  be a primitive third root of unity.

Now the probability that  $\sigma(S_1\cup S_2)$  is a multiple of  $3$  is \begin{align*}
\sum_T P(S_1\cup S_2 = T)1_{3|\sigma(S_1, S_2)} &= \sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|}1_{3|\sigma(T)}
&= \sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|}(\frac{1 + \omega^{\sigma(T)} + \omega^{2\sigma(T)}}{3})
&= \frac{1}{3}\sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|} + \frac{1}{3}\sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|}\omega^{\sigma(T)} + \frac{1}{3}\sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|}\omega^{2\sigma(T)}.
\end{align*}

The first term in this sum is clearly  $\frac{1}{3}$ . As for the second term, we have \begin{align*}
\frac{1}{3}\sum_T (\frac{3}{4})^{|T|} (\frac{1}{4})^{9 - |T|}\omega^{\sigma(T)} &= \frac{1}{3}\sum_T\prod_t (\frac{1}{4}1_{t\notin T} + \frac{3\omega^t}{4}1_{t\in T})
&= \frac{1}{3}\prod_{t\in\{1,\dots, 9\}}(\frac{1}{4} + \frac{3\omega^t}{4})
&= \frac{1}{3}((\frac{1}{4} + \frac{3}{4}) (\frac{1}{4} + \frac{3\omega}{4}) (\frac{1}{4} + \frac{3\omega^2}{4}))^3
&= \frac{1}{3}((\frac{1}{4})^3 - (\frac{3}{4})^3)
&= \frac{1}{3}\frac{-26}{64}.
\end{align*}

Similarly, the third term is also  $\frac{1}{3}\frac{-26}{64}$ .

Then the total sum is  $\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{3}\frac{-26}{64} + \frac{1}{3}\frac{-26}{64} = \frac{1}{3}\frac{64 - 26 - 26}{64} = \frac{1}{3}\frac{12}{64} = \boxed{\frac{1}{16}}$ , and we are done.

