From here, it is quite easy to find an algorithm that terminates in  $5n + O(1)$  steps. 
After the first  $n - 1$  steps, we have  $|\mathcal{V}_0| = 1$  and  $|\mathcal{V}_{1}| = n - 1$ . Now doing the same for the  $n - 1$  vertices in  $\mathcal{V}_1$ ,  we could reduce this to  $|\mathcal{V}_1| = 2$  by adding  $n - 3$  edges (as it is possible that the last two edges are already connected in the previous round, and for any  $|\mathcal{V}_1| \ge 3$ , since the first graph is not a tree, we can always connect edges between two vertices that hasn't been connected before, which reduces  $\mathcal{V}_1$  by one.) Now, we have  $|\mathcal{V}_0| = 1$  and  $|\mathcal{V}_1| = 2$ . There are several possible cases. 


- If the unique ingoing edge of the vertices in  $\mathcal{V}_0$  is not a member of  $\mathcal{V}_1$ , 
