<details><summary>Solution</summary>Define  $A',B',C'$  as the intersections of  $\overline{PA_1},\overline{PB_1},\overline{PC_1}$  with  $\odot(ABC)$  respectively. We state the following claim which essentially finishes the problem.**Claim:** One of the ratios  $\frac{PA_1}{A_1A'},\frac{PB_1}{B_1B'},\frac{PC_1}{C_1C'}$  is  $\geq 1$   

*Proof:* By sine rule stuff we get that 
\begin{align*}
& \frac{PA_1}{A_1A'} = \frac{\sin \angle PBC \cdot \sin \angle BCA}{\sin \angle BPA \cdot \sin \angle CAP}  
& \frac{PB_1}{B_1B'} = \frac{\sin \angle PCA \cdot \sin \angle BAC}{\sin \angle BPC \cdot \sin \angle PBA}  
& \frac{PC_1}{C_1C'}= \frac{\sin \angle PAB \cdot \sin \angle ABC}{\sin \angle CPA \cdot \sin \angle PCB}  
\end{align*}
and also we have that 
\begin{align*}
& \frac{PA_1}{A_1A'} = \frac{\sin \angle PCB \cdot \sin \angle CBA}{\sin \angle CPA \cdot \sin \angle BAP}  
& \frac{PB_1}{B_1B'} = \frac{\sin \angle PAC \cdot \sin \angle BCA}{\sin \angle BPA \cdot \sin \angle PBC}  
& \frac{PC_1}{C_1C'}= \frac{\sin \angle PBA \cdot \sin \angle BAC}{\sin \angle CPB \cdot \sin \angle PCA}  
\end{align*}
Multiplying stuff from these equations yields the following few equations. 

Assume the contradiction. Multiplying equations among these 6 equations yields the following results.

\begin{align*}
&\left(\frac{\sin \angle BAC}{\sin \angle BPC}\right)^2 < 1 
&\left(\frac{\sin \angle BCA}{\sin \angle BPA}\right)^2 < 1 
&\left(\frac{\sin \angle ABC}{\sin \angle APC}\right)^2 < 1 
\end{align*}
but then this must imply that  $\angle BAC < \pi - \angle BPC$  and cyclic inequalities. But notice that summing this inequalities yields that  $\pi < 3\pi- 2\pi$  which is clearly wrong. With this we achieve the desired contradicition  $\qquad \blacksquare$</details>

