Let  $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$  be different real numbers. Prove that 
\[\sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant n} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{1-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } n \text { is even; } 
1, & \text { if } n \text { is odd. }
\end{array}\right.\]

candidates:
For distinct real numbers  $x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n$  define 
	  $$ f(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) := \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{1-x_ix_j}{x_i-x_j} $$  	We will use induction on  $n$ . If  $n=2$ , then
	  $$ f(x_1, x_2) = \frac{1-x_1x_2}{x_1-x_2} + \frac{1-x_2x_1}{x_2-x_1} =  \frac{1-x_1x_2}{x_1-x_2} - \frac{1-x_1x_2}{x_1-x_2} = 0 $$  	Suppose the claim is true up to  $n$  and let's prove it for  $n$ . Consider
	  $$ g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) = \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\left(1 + \frac{1-x_ix_j}{x_i-x_j}\right) $$  	After expanding product on the right hand side we get
\begin{align*}
g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) &= \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\sum_{\substack{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_ki\not\in\{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_k\}}}\prod_{j\in \{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_k\}}\frac{1-x_ix_j}{x_i-x_j}
		&=\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{\substack{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_ki\not\in\{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_k\}}}\prod_{j\in \{s_1, s_2, \dots, s_k\}}\frac{1-x_ix_j}{x_i-x_j}
		&=\displaystyle\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\displaystyle\sum_{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_{k+1}}f(x_{i_1}, x_{i_2}, \dots, x_{i_{k+1}})
	\end{align*}
	Then by induction hypothesis
	\begin{align*}
	g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) &= f(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) + \displaystyle\sum_{\substack{k=0k\text{ even}}}^{n-2}\sum_{i_1, i_2, \dots, i_{k+1}}1 =f(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) + \displaystyle\sum_{\substack{k=0k\text{ even}}}^{n-2}\binom{n}{k+1}
	&= f(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) + \begin{cases} 2^{n-1},&\text{if }n\text{ is even;}
	2^{n-1} - 1,&\text{if }n\text{ is odd.}
	\end{cases}
	\end{align*}
	On the other hand
	\begin{align*}
		g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) &= \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\left(1 + \frac{1-x_ix_j}{x_i-x_j}\right) = \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{(1 + x_i)(1-x_j)}{x_i-x_j}=\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left((1 + x_i)^{n-1}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{1-x_j}{x_i-x_j}\right)
	\end{align*}
	Using binomial expansion we get
	\begin{align*}
	g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) &=\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}\binom{n-1}{r}x_i^r\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{1-x_j}{x_i-x_j}=\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}\left(\binom{n-1}{r}\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(x_i^r\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{1-x_j}{x_i-x_j}\right)\right)
	\end{align*}
	By Lagrange Interpolation Theorem we know that
	  $$ t^r = \displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(x_i^{r}\displaystyle\prod_{j\neq i}\frac{t-x_j}{x_i-x_j}\right) $$  	for all  $0 \leq r \leq n-1$ .  So by plugging  $t=1$  in this identity and using it in above equation we get
	  $$ 	g(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n)=\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}\binom{n-1}{r}=2^{n-1} $$  	Combining this with above equality we get
	  $$ f(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_n) = \begin{cases} 0,&\text{if }n\text{ is even;}
	1,&\text{if }n\text{ is odd.}
	\end{cases} $$  	and this completes our induction.

This is a proof without using Lagrange Interpolation.
<details><summary>Solution</summary>We will prove that   $$ \sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant n} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } n \text { is even; } 
x^{n-1}, & \text { if } n \text { is odd }
\end{array}\right. $$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$  by induction.
The basis step when  $n=1,2$  is obvious.
<details><summary>Inductive step</summary>Let   $$ \sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant m} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } m \text { is even; } 
x^{m-1}, & \text { if } m \text { is odd }
\end{array}\right. $$  for all  $x \in \mathbb{R}$ .
Consider when  $k \in \{1,2,3,\dots,m+1\}$  
\begin{align*}
\sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant {m+1}} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{{x_k}^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}} &=x_k^m +\sum_{\substack{1 \leqslant i \leqslant {m+1}  i\neq k}}\prod_{j \neq i} \frac{{x_k}^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}
&=x_k^m -x_k \cdot \sum_{\substack{1 \leqslant i \leqslant {m+1}  i\neq k}}\prod_{j \neq i,k} \frac{{x_k}^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}
&=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
x_k^m-x_k \cdot x_k^{m-1}, & \text { if } m \text { is odd; } 
x_k^m-x_k \cdot 0 ,& \text { if } m \text { is even }
\end{array}\right. \text{(Using inductive hypothesis.)}
&=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } m+1 \text { is even; } 
x_k^m,& \text { if } m+1 \text { is odd. }
\end{array}\right.
\end{align*}
Thus   $$ \sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant m+1} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } m+1 \text { is even; } 
x^{m}, & \text { if } m+1 \text { is odd }
\end{array}\right. $$  for all  $x \in \{x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{m+1}\}$ .
It is obvious that this is also true for all  $x \in \{x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{m+1},-x_1,-x_2,\dots,-x_{m+1}\}$ .
Consider the case when it does not exist  $i\neq j,\ i,j \in \{1,2,3,\dots,m+1\}$   such that  $x_i+x_j=0$ .
Note that there is at least  $2m+1$  different numbers in  $\{x_1,x_2,\dots,x_{m+1},-x_1,-x_2,\dots,-x_{m+1}\}$ and   $$ \sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant m+1} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}} $$   is a polynomial of degree at most  $2m$ .
Hence   $$ \sum_{1 \leqslant i \leqslant m+1} \prod_{j \neq i} \frac{x^2-x_{i} x_{j}}{x_{i}-x_{j}}=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
0, & \text { if } m+1 \text { is even; } 
x^{m}, & \text { if } m+1 \text { is odd }
\end{array}\right. $$   for all  $x\in \mathbb{R}$ .
The latter case can be done by continuity.</details>
Put  $x=1$ . This completes the proof.</details>

The idea is that if we turn the given into a rational function of the form  $\frac{P(x)}{Q(x)}$ , then  $P(x)$  has degree  $\binom{n}{2} + (n - 1)$ , and has a bajillion roots.

Apply the substitution  $x_i = \frac{y_i - 1}{y_i + 1}$ . Notice that if  $x_i = 1$  for some  $i$ , then any terms containing  $x_i$  become  $\frac{1 - x_ix_j}{x_i - x_j} = \frac{1 - x_j}{1 - x_j} = 1$ , so we can just induct down and thus  $x_i - 1$  is divisible by  $P(x)$  or  $P(x) - 1$ . Henceforth assume none of them are  $1$ . 

This transforms the problem into evaluating \begin{align*}
    \sum_{1 \leq i \leq n} \prod_{j \neq i, 1 \leq j \leq n} \frac{y_i + y_j}{y_i - y_j}.
\end{align*} 

Now, the key claim is that  $\textbf{Claim: }$   $(y_i + y_j)$  divides  $P(x)$  or  $P(x) - 1$  depending on parity for all  $i < j$ , so  $\binom{n}{2}$  total.

