Let  $k=|A|$ , and define  $I=\{2i + \lfloor i\sqrt{2} \rfloor : i \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}\}$ . Notice that  $H$  and  $I$  partition  $\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$  because they are Beatty sequences with  $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}+ \frac{1}{2+\sqrt{2}}=1$ . Suppose that for all  $a,b \in A$ ,  $a-b \notin H$ , i.e.  $a-b \in I$ . 

Next, let the elements of  $A$  be  $a_1 < a_2 < \cdots < a_k$ . Define the differences  $d_1=a_2-a_1, d_2=a_3-a_2, \cdots d_{k-1}=a_k-a_{k-1}$ . By the assumption,  $d_1,d_2, \cdots d_{k-1} \in I$ , so we may write  $d_1= 2x_1+\lfloor x_1\sqrt{2}\rfloor, d_2= 2x_2+\lfloor x_2\sqrt{2}\rfloor, \cdots d_{k-1}= 2x_{k-1}+\lfloor x_{k-1}\sqrt{2}\rfloor$ . 

<span style="color:#00f">**Claim:**</span>  $\{x_1\sqrt{2}\}+ \{x_2\sqrt{2}\} + \cdots \{x_{k-1}\sqrt{2}\} < 1$ *Proof:* Suppose otherwise, and let  $j$  be the smallest index such that  $\{x_1\sqrt{2}\}+ \{x_2\sqrt{2}\} + \cdots \{x_{j}\sqrt{2}\} \ge1$ . By the minimality of  $j$  and the fact that each  $\{x_i\sqrt{2}\} <1$ , we must have that  $1 \le \{x_1\sqrt{2}\}+ \{x_2\sqrt{2}\} + \cdots \{x_{j}\sqrt{2}\} < 2$ . Consider the following:
\begin{align*}
a_{j+1}-a_1 &= d_1+d_2 + \cdots + d_j  &= 2(x_1+x_2+\cdots +x_j) + \lfloor x_1\sqrt{2}\rfloor + \lfloor x_2\sqrt{2}\rfloor + \cdots \lfloor x_j \sqrt{2}\rfloor  &= 2(x_1+x_2+\cdots +x_j) + \sqrt{2}(x_1+x_2+\cdots x_j) -\{x_1\sqrt{2}\}+ \{x_2\sqrt{2}\} + \cdots \{x_{j}\sqrt{2}\}  &=  2(x_1+x_2+\cdots +x_j) + \lfloor \sqrt{2}(x_1+x_2+\cdots x_j) \rfloor - 1
\end{align*}
This means  $a_{j+1}-a_1$  is  $1$  less than an element of  $I$ , so it can't be in  $I$  as any two elements of  $I$  clearly differ by at least  $2$ . This is a contradiction and we are done.

Now, observe that  $d_i > 2x_i$  for each  $i$ , so   $a_k-a_1=d_1+d_2 + \cdots d_{k-1}< n \implies x_1+x_2+\cdots x_{k-1} < \frac{n}{2}$ .

 Furthermore, by Liouville's theorem, there exists a constant  $r$  such that for all integers  $p,q$ ,  $|\sqrt{2}-\frac{p}{q}| > \frac{r}{q^2} \implies |q\sqrt{2} - p| > \frac{r}{q} \implies \{q\sqrt{2}\}>\frac{r}{q}$ Hence, we have the following by Cauchy-Schwartz: 
\begin{align*}
\frac{n}{2} &> (\{x_1\sqrt{2}\}+ \{x_2\sqrt{2}\} + \cdots \{x_{k-1}\sqrt{2}\}) \cdot (x_1+x_2+\cdots x_{k-1})  &> (\frac{r}{x_1} + \frac{r}{x_2} + \cdots + \frac{r}{x_{k-1}}) \cdot (x_1+x_2+\cdots x_{k-1})   &> (r(k-1))^2
\end{align*}
From this we can see that if  $k=C\sqrt{n}$ , where  $C$  is a large enough constant, the RHS of the inequality will exceed the LHS. This is a contradiction so our assumption that  $a-b \in I$  for all  $a,b \in A$  was false, as desired.

For any  $A=\{a_1<a_2<\dots<a_{k+1}\}$ , let  $d_i=a_{i+1}-a_i$ . We have \[\lfloor x_i\sqrt 2\rfloor<d_i<\lfloor (x_i+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor\] for some  $x_i$  for all  $i$ . Since consecutive elements of  $H$  can only differ by  $1$  or  $2$ , we have  $\lfloor x_i\sqrt 2\rfloor,d_i,\lfloor (x_i+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor$  are consecutive, and in particular  $d_i=\lfloor x_i\sqrt 2\rfloor+1$ .

Now consider any  $a,b\notin H$  such that  $a+b\notin H$ . Write
\begin{align*}
    \lfloor x\sqrt 2\rfloor<a<\lfloor (x+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor
    \lfloor y\sqrt 2\rfloor<b<\lfloor (y+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor
\end{align*}
We now have
\begin{align*}
    &\lfloor (x+y)\sqrt 2\rfloor && \lfloor (x+y+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor
    \leq &\lfloor x\sqrt 2\rfloor+\lfloor y\sqrt 2\rfloor+1 & \geq & \lfloor (x+y+2)\sqrt 2\rfloor-2
    <&a+b & \geq &\lfloor (x+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor+\lfloor (y+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor-2
    && \geq & a+b
\end{align*}
Then  $\lfloor (x+y)\sqrt 2\rfloor$  is the largest member of  $H$  less than  $a+b$ .

It then follows that for any  $1\leq s\leq t\leq k$  we have \[\lfloor (x_s+x_{s+1}+\dots+x_t)\sqrt 2\rfloor<d_s+d_{s+1}+\dots+d_t<\lfloor (x_s+x_{s+1}+\dots+x_t+1)\sqrt 2\rfloor\] where we have  $d_s+d_{s+1}+\dots+d_t=a_{t+1}-a_s$ . In particular, we have
\begin{align*}
    &d_1+d_2+\dots+d_k=\lfloor (x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k)\sqrt 2\rfloor+1
    \implies& \lfloor x_1\sqrt 2\rfloor+\lfloor x_2\sqrt 2\rfloor+\dots+\lfloor x_k\sqrt 2\rfloor+k=\lfloor (x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k)\sqrt 2\rfloor+1
    \implies& \lfloor -x_1\sqrt 2\rfloor+\lfloor -x_2\sqrt 2\rfloor+\dots+\lfloor -x_k\sqrt 2\rfloor=\lfloor -(x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k)\sqrt 2\rfloor
    \implies&\{-x_1\sqrt 2\}+\{-x_2\sqrt 2\}+\dots+\{-x_k\sqrt 2\}=\{-(x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k)\sqrt 2\}
    \implies& \{-x_1\sqrt 2\}+\{-x_2\sqrt 2\}+\dots+\{-x_k\sqrt 2\}<1
\end{align*}

We note now that there exists a constant  $c$  such that \[a_{k+1}\leq n\impliedby x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k\leq cn\] It now suffices to show that there exists a constant  $C$  such that for any  $n$  and  $k\geq C\sqrt n$ , for any positive integers  $x_1,x_2,\dots,x_k$  with  $x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k\leq n$ , we have \[\{-\sqrt 2 x_1\}+\{-\sqrt 2 x_2\}+\dots+\{-\sqrt 2 x_k\}\geq 1\] Now note that for any  $x$ , we have \[\{-x\sqrt 2\}=-\lfloor -x\sqrt 2\rfloor-x\sqrt 2=\frac{{\lfloor -x\sqrt 2\rfloor}^2-2x^2}{-\lfloor -x\sqrt 2\rfloor+x\sqrt 2}\geq \frac{1}{-\lfloor x\sqrt 2\rfloor+x\sqrt 2}>\frac{1}{x\sqrt 2}\] Then taking  $C=\sqrt[4]{2}$ , we have for  $k\geq C\sqrt n$  that
\begin{align*}
    &\{-x_1\sqrt 2\}+\{-x_2\sqrt 2\}+\dots+\{-x_k\sqrt 2\}
    >&\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\left(\frac{1}{x_1}+\frac{1}{x_2}+\dots+\frac{1}{x_k}\right)
    \geq&\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\left(\frac{k^2}{x_1+x_2+\dots+x_k}\right)
    \geq&\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\left(\frac{k^2}{n}\right)
    \geq&\frac{C^2}{\sqrt 2}
    =&1
\end{align*}
as desired.

