Let  $\alpha \geq 1$  be a real number. Hephaestus and Poseidon play a turn-based game on an infinite grid of unit squares. Before the game starts, Poseidon chooses a finite number of cells to be flooded. Hephaestus is building a levee, which is a subset of unit edges of the grid (called walls) forming a connected, non-self-intersecting path or loop*.

The game then begins with Hephaestus moving first. On each of Hephaestus’s turns, he adds one or more walls to the levee, as long as the total length of the levee is at most  $\alpha n$  after his  $n$ th turn. On each of Poseidon’s turns, every cell which is adjacent to an already flooded cell and with no wall between them becomes flooded as well. Hephaestus wins if the levee forms a closed loop such that all flooded cells are contained in the interior of the loop — hence stopping the flood and saving the world. For which  $\alpha$  can Hephaestus guarantee victory in a finite number of turns no matter how Poseidon chooses the initial cells to flood?
[rule]
The answer is  $\alpha \ge 3$ . We just need to prove that  $3$  is sufficient for Hephaestus to win the game, and that  $2$  is insufficient.**Part 1: Sufficient.** Let  $\alpha=3$ .

Consider a diamond, defined as the set of cells for which, when assigned a coordinate  $(x,y)$  in the usual way, satisfies  $|x-a|+|y-b|\le c$  for some positive integers  $a$ ,  $b$ , and  $c$ . Let  $c$  be the size of the diamond. Since the number of flooded cells in the beginning is finite, there exists a diamond of finite size  $d$  that encompasses every flooded cell. Assume  $d>100$ , and that all of these cells in this diamond are flooded to begin, which does not affect the result of the problem.

[asy]
filldraw((-5,0)--(-5,1)--(-4,1)--(-4,2)--(-3,2)--(-3,3)--(-2,3)--(-2,4)--(-1,4)--(-1,5)--(0,5)--(0,4)--(1,4)--(1,3)--(2,3)--(2,2)--(3,2)--(3,1)--(4,1)--(4,0)--(3,0)--(3,-1)--(2,-1)--(2,-2)--(1,-2)--(1,-3)--(0,-3)--(0,-4)--(-1,-4)--(-1,-3)--(-2,-3)--(-2,-2)--(-3,-2)--(-3,-1)--(-4,-1)--(-4,0)--cycle,lightcyan,black+linewidth(1));
label("A diamond of size  $4$ ", (-0.5,-6));

filldraw((15,0)--(20,5)--(25,0)--(20,-5)--cycle,lightcyan,black+linewidth(1));
label("A diamond of arbitrary size", (20,-6));
[/asy]

At the bottom-most point of the diamond, begin building a horizontal wall. 

[asy]
fill((4,1)--(4,0)--(3,0)--(3,-1)--(2,-1)--(2,-2)--(1,-2)--(1,-3)--(0,-3)--(0,-4)--(-1,-4)--(-1,-3)--(-2,-3)--(-2,-2)--(-3,-2)--(-3,-1)--(-4,-1)--(-4,0)--(-5,0)--(-5,1)--cycle,lightcyan);
draw((4,1)--(4,0)--(3,0)--(3,-1)--(2,-1)--(2,-2)--(1,-2)--(1,-3)--(0,-3)--(0,-4)--(-1,-4)--(-1,-3)--(-2,-3)--(-2,-2)--(-3,-2)--(-3,-1)--(-4,-1)--(-4,0)--(-5,0)--(-5,1),black);
draw((-2,-4)--(1,-4),brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

Since  $\alpha = 3$ , the speed at which this wall is being built will always exceed the speed at which the water expands. For the next  $6d$  turns, expand the wall in the horizontal direction, building a total of  $9d$  in each direction. 

[asy]
filldraw((-6,0)--(-7,1)--(0,8)--(7,1)--(6,0)--cycle,lightcyan,black+linewidth(1));
filldraw((0,0)--(-1,1)--(0,2)--(1,1)--cycle,cyan,black+linewidth(1));
draw((-2,1)--(-6,1),arrow=Arrow(),heavycyan);
draw((2,1)--(6,1),arrow=Arrow(),heavycyan);
draw((0,3)--(0,7),arrow=Arrow(),heavycyan);
draw((-9,0)--(9,0),brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

The water now forms a truncated diamond whose shape looks more like an actual diamond. Consider extending the longest sides of this diamond until they intersect the wall. This forms an isosceles right triangle. It doesn't hurt now to flood all of these squares as well.

[asy]
filldraw((-8,0)--(0,8)--(8,0)--cycle,lightcyan,black+linewidth(1));
draw((-9,0)--(9,0),brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

Now, there is an extra distance  $d$  between the edge of the water and the edge of wall. We can begin building the wall from the edges vertically. Build the walls up for the next  $40d$  turns, building each side up  $60d$ .

[asy]
filldraw((-9,0)--(-9,39)--(0,48)--(9,39)--(9,0)--cycle,lightcyan,black+linewidth(1));
filldraw((-6,0)--(-7,1)--(0,8)--(7,1)--(6,0)--cycle,cyan,black+linewidth(1));
draw((0,18)--(0,38),arrow=Arrow(),heavycyan);
draw((-9,60)--(-9,0)--(9,0)--(9,60),brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

At this point, we have encompassed the water on three sides. The final side will take  $6d$  turns to close, but the diamond's height is currently  $48d$ , because it started at  $2d$  and grew once per turn for  $46d$  turns. After the  $6d$  turns are used, the diamond's height is  $54d$ , but the height of the walls built was  $60d$ , so the water never escaped.

[asy]
filldraw((-9,60)--(-9,0)--(9,0)--(9,60)--cycle,lightcyan,brown+linewidth(4));
label("Hephaestus saves the world :)",(0,-10));
[/asy]**Part 2: Necessary.** Let  $\alpha = 2$ .

Suppose Hephaestus can achieve this goal. Then, consider the way to achieve this that forces the number of levees built to stay constant but reaches maximal area. Consider any concavity in the closed chessboard polygon.

[asy]
filldraw((1,0)--(0,0)--(0,1)--(-2,1)--(-2,-2)--(1,-2)--cycle,lightcyan,brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

The presumptions made in the diagram are not restrictive; this argument works for all concavities. Consider the two levees adjacent to the point of concavity. If they were built in the same move, then replacing them with the following configuration will not allow the water to leave. 

[asy]
filldraw((1,0)--(0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5)--(0,0.5)--(0,1)--(-2,1)--(-2,-2)--(1,-2)--cycle,lightcyan,brown+linewidth(4));
fill((0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5)--(0,0.5)--(0,0)--cycle, cyan);
draw((0.5,0)--(0.5,0.5)--(0,0.5),brown+linewidth(4));
[/asy]

If the original two levees were stopping the water from flowing to another spot, note that the only water path that changing the levees allows is a path that is already covered by another path.

A similar argument can be made if one of the levees was built in an earlier move than the other; in this case, by the time the water gets to the added cell, the other levee must be in place already, or else the water would've escaped in the original configuration. Therefore, the water is still contained if we fill in the concavity.

Continue to fill in concavities in this manner, and you receive the only convex chessboard polygon: a rectangle. Unfortunately, after  $t$  turns, the maximum rectangle will have diameter (that is, maximum taxicab distance)  $t-2$  while the water after  $t-1$  turns will have reached diameter  $t-1$ , which is too much to contain.

