I claim the answer is all positive even numbers. Any even number  $2k$  can be attained by setting  $y = k, x = 1$  and  $z = 1$ . We now show that all integers of this form are even.

First, we note that if  $d \mid x, y$  and  $z$ , then we could write  $x = da$ ,  $y = db$  and  $z = dc$ , and we would have  $$ \frac{\operatorname{lcm}(da, db) + \operatorname{lcm}(db, dc)}{\operatorname{lcm}(da, dc)} = \frac{\operatorname{lcm}(a, b) + \operatorname{lcm}(b, c)}{\operatorname{lcm}(a, c)}. $$  Thus we may suppose without loss of generality that  $\gcd(x, y, z) = 1$ . 

With this condition, we can write  $x = d_{xy} d_{xz}a$ ,  $y = d_{xy} d_{yz} b$  and  $z = d_{xz} d_{yz} c$ , so that  $\gcd(x, y) = d_{xy}$  etc. This is because if  $d_{xy}$  and  $d_{xz}$  were not relatively prime, then  $x, y$  and  $z$  would have a common factor.

This setup also implies that  $a$ ,  $b$  and  $c$  are pairwise coprime, so we have
\begin{align*}
	\frac{\operatorname{lcm}(x, y)+\operatorname{lcm}(y, z)}{\operatorname{lcm}(x, z)}
&=\frac{\operatorname{lcm}(d_{xy} d_{xz}a, d_{xy} d_{yz} b)+\operatorname{lcm}(d_{xy} d_{yz} b, d_{xz} d_{yz} c)}{\operatorname{lcm}(d_{xy} d_{xz}a, d_{xz} d_{yz} c)} 
&= \frac{d_{xy} d_{xz} d_{yz}ab + d_{xy} d_{xz} d_{yz}bc}{d_{xy} d_{xz} d_{yz}ac} 
&= \frac{ab + bc}{ac} = b \left(\frac{a + c}{ac}\right) 
&= b\left(\frac{1}{a} + \frac{1}{c}\right),
\end{align*}
and thus as this is an integer and  $\gcd(a, c) = 1$ , we must have  $a = c = 1$ , and so this is equal to  $2b$ , which is even. **Note**. A slight variation on this proof is to note that if a prime  $p \mid x$  and  $p \nmid y$  then  $p \mid z$ , and also if  $p \mid x$  but  $p \nmid z$  then  $p \mid y$ . This comes from the fact that the resulting expression must be an integer. We can use this do deduce that  $a$  (and hence  $c$ ) in the solution above must be  $1$  (as there can be no prime  $p$  dividing it that does not also divide  $z$  or  $y$ )!

