Factor  $P$  into irreducible factors such that \[P(x) = c\cdot x^{e_0}\prod_{k = 1}^{n}Q_k(x)^{e_k}\] and  $Q_k$  are irreducible and relatively prime to each other as well as to  $x.$ \begin{lemma}
    If  $f\in\mathbb Z[x]$  is irreducible, then for infinitely many primes  $p,$  there exists  $n$  such that  $\nu_p(f(n))=1.$ \end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Since  $f$  is irreducible, it follows that  $f$  and  $f'$  have no common factors. By Bezout, there exists  $a, b\in\mathbb Z[x]$  such that  $af + bf' = C$  for some  $C \in \mathbb Z.$  By Schurs, we may pick some  $p > |C|$  such that  $p\mid f(n)$  for some  $n.$  By Hensels, there exists  $n$  such that  $p^2 \mid f(n).$  Since  $p\nmid C,$  we have  $p\nmid f'(n).$  By Binomial theorem,  $f(n + p) \equiv f(n) + pf'(n) \equiv pf'(n)\pmod{p^2},$  finishing our claim. 
\end{proof}
Pick primes  $p_0 < p_1 < p_2 < \dots < p_n < q$  such that they exceed  $c, \max e_i,$  the  $\gcd$  of pairwise polynomials(by bezout). By CRT, we may pick  $N$  such that 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item  $\nu_{p_i}(f_i(N)) = 1$  from our claim
    \item  $\nu_{p_0}(N) = q$  from  $N\equiv p_0^q\pmod{p_0^{q + 1}}$     \item  $p_i\nmid f_j(N)$  unless  $i = j$     \item  $N$  is a perfect power(from fermat little theorem)
\end{enumerate}
Thus,  $\nu_{p_i}(f(N)) = e_i$  other than  $\nu_{p_0}(f(N)) = qe_0$  implying that they all have some common divisor that is greater than 1. This finishes.

