\end{align*}
Thus the sum decreases monotonically after each move but clearly the sum is bounded below by  $\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2} \cdot \text{Min height}$  so it cannot keep on decreasing forever  $\implies$  Carl must stop after a finite number of moves.**Part b.** Consider the situation when Carl stops. Assume for contradiction that there is atleast a pair of books  $(B_i , B_{i+1})$  such that  $\mathcal W(i)>\mathcal W(i+1)$  but notice that in such a situation  $\mathcal H(i) > \mathcal H(i+1)$  must hold (else another move can be made) but this implies that books  $(B_i , B_{i+1})$  were never moved before (the operation cannot be performed under such conditions), i.e they are present where they are ever since the starting, but this contradicts our given condition that every book was arranged in the increasing order of height from left to right.

solution_md:
Nice and beautiful problem! Unfortunately I heard it dies to Farey Fractions or something...

We replace  $100$  with any positive integer  $n$ . First, we transform the problem into the following one:

<span style="color:#f00">**Transformed Problem:**</span>Suppose there are  $n$  vectors  $\mathbf{v}_i=(a_i,b_i)$  in the plane with  $a_i,b_i\ge 0$ . Call a pair vectors  $(\mathbf{v}_i,\mathbf{v}_j)$  *friendly* if the area of the triangle formed by the two vectors with the origin is  $1/2$  (i.e. the area of the triangle with vertices  $(0,0),(a_i,b_i),(a_j,b_j)$  is  $1/2$ ). Determine the maximum number of friendly pairs over all possible choice of the  $n$  vectors.

This problem is the same because of the Shoelace formula (I was actually motivated by determinants:
\[\begin{vmatrix}
a_i & a_j
b_i & b_j
\end{vmatrix}=a_ib_j-a_jb_i\]
gives the area of the parallelogram formed by  $\mathbf{v}_i$  and  $\mathbf{v}_j$ , but Shoelace is equivalent).

Solving this problem felt very similar to USOJMO 2020/3, in that we need to find a construction but also bound. We claim that the answer is  $2n-3$ , so the final answer for  $n=100$  is  $\boxed{197}$ .

<span style="font-size:150%">**Part 1: Construction**</span>

We provide a construction that, for any  $n$ , gives at least  $2n-3$  friendly pairs. Not too difficult if you play with small cases; we recurse. First note that  $(1,0)$  and  $(0,1)$  is a valid base case for  $n=1$  as it gives  $1$  friendly pair, and to create a construction for  $n=3$ , we add in the vector  $(1,1)$ , giving a total of  $3$  friendly pairs. This is the main idea of the induction: given vectors
\[\mathbf{v}_1,\mathbf{v}_2,\dots,\mathbf{v}_n,\]
with at least  $2n-3$  friendly pairs, and where  $\mathbf{v}_{n-1}$  and  $\mathbf{v}_n$  are friendly (you can check that this holds in the base case and after the inductive step), we can induct up to the  $n+1$  case by adding in the vector  $\mathbf{v}_{n+1}=\mathbf{v}_n+\mathbf{v}_{n-1}$ . The following picture reveals that  $(\mathbf{v}_{n+1},\mathbf{v}_{n-1})$  and  $(\mathbf{v}_{n+1},\mathbf{v}_n)$  are both friendly pairs:
[asy]
draw((0,0)--(2,4), Arrow);
draw((0,0)--(5,2), Arrow);
draw((5,2)--(7,6), dashed);
draw((2,4)--(7,6), dashed);
draw((2,4)--(5,2), dashed);
draw((0,0)--(7,6), Arrow);

label(" $\mathbf{v}_{n+1}$ ", (7,6), NE);
label(" $\mathbf{v}_n$ ", (2,4), NW);
label(" $\mathbf{v}_{n-1}$ ", (5,2), SE);
[/asy]

The parallelogram ensures that if the area of the triangle formed by  $\mathbf{v}_n$  and  $\mathbf{v}_{n-1}$  is  $1/2$ , then so are those formed by  $\mathbf{v}_{n+1}$  with both  $\mathbf{v}_n$  and  $\mathbf{v}_{n-1}$ . Therefore, we gain at least two friendly pairs going from  $n$  to  $n+1$ , which finishes.

<span style="font-size:150%">**Part 2: Upper Bound**</span>

The idea here is the following: we want to show that the jump is *exactly*  $2$  when you go from  $n$  to  $n+1$ . In other words, if the answer to the problem is  $f(n)$ , we want to show that we can't get  $f(n+1)\ge f(n)+3$ . 

How can we do this? Well, we know that if we remove a single vector from a group of  $n+1$ , the friendly pairs between the other  $n$  are not affected. Therefore, **it suffices show that there exists some vector which is only part of two friendly pairs.**

Which vector should we choose? Well, we are inspired by our previous construction: the two vectors sum to a large vector which connects to two beneath it. So it makes sense that we should pick some sort of *maximal* vector. Indeed, consider a collection of  $n+1$  vectors which achieves  $f(n+1)$  friendly pairs. Then, here's the key claim:**<span style="color:#f00">Claim: </span>**Consider the vector  $\mathbf{v}_m$  with maximal  $a_m+b_m$ . This vector is only a part of at most two friendly pairs. Indeed, there exists at most one solution to either  $a_mb_i+a_ib_m=1$  or  $a_mb_i+a_ib_m=-1$ .

*Proof:* Quite number theoretical. First note that if  $\gcd(a_m,b_m)\ge 1$  then we clearly get no friendly pairs, so assume  $\gcd(a_m,b_m)=1$ . Given any solution to  $a_mb_i+a_ib_m=1$ , call it  $(a,b)$ , we know that because of this relatively prime condition, the solution set is  $(a+ka_m,b+kb_m)$ . By the maximality of  $a_m+b_m$ , there is at most one solution. Here's the picture that makes this clear:

[asy]
draw((-1,0)--(10,0), Arrow);
draw((0,-1)--(0,10), Arrow);
draw((8,0)--(0,8), cyan+dashed);
dot((5,3));
label(" $\mathbf{v}_m$ ", (5,3), NE);
draw((0,0)--(5,3), Arrow);

draw((-4,-4/5)--(8,32/5), green+0.5);
dot((2,14/5));
dot((-3,-1/5), red);
dot((7,29/5), red);
[/asy]

The black dot is the solution to  $a_mb_i+a_ib_m=1$  (if any exists), and the red dots are the nearest neighbors satisfying the equation, but they lie outside the right isosceles triangle. The same holds for  $a_mb_i+a_ib_m=-1$ , so we indeed get at most  $2$  friendly pairs involving  $\mathbf{v}_m$ .  $\square$ Hence,  $f(n+1)\le f(n)+2$ , as desired.

Combining both the construction and the upper bound shows that  $197$  is indeed optimal.**Remark:** ***tree3*** notes that transforming the problem isn't even necessary, applying the solution above directly to the problem works as well! However, this transformed problem made it a lot easier for me to think about what was going on as well as play with small cases.

