[/asy]

Let  $\ell_1$  intersect  $AB$  and  $BC$  at  $X$  and  $Y$ , respectively. It suffices to show that there is at most one lattice point on segment  $XY$ , since then the analogous statement for  $\ell_2$  holds and we are done.

First, note that  $\gcd(a_i, b_i) \mid |a_iy - b_jx|$  for all integers  $x, y$  so if  $\gcd(a_i, b_i) \neq 1$  we are done. Thus assume  $\gcd(a_i, b_i) = 1$ . This means that there are no lattice points in the interior of  $AP$ .

Because  $XY \parallel AP$ , if  $XY < AP$ , then clearly segment  $XY$  contains at most one lattice point. But we have  $$ \frac{XY}{AP} = \frac{BX}{AB} < 1 $$  so we are done.



Thus the claim is proven. Now, take each pair  $(i, j)$  with  $i < j$  and  $|a_ib_j - a_jb_i| = 1$  and write the number  $j$  on a list. By the claim, when the list is complete, it contains each integer at most twice. Further, it clearly doesn't contain the integer  $1$ , and contains the integer  $2$  at most once, so we have  $$ N \le 2(n-2) + 0 + 1 = 2n-3 $$  and we are done.</details>

