By Reim,  $ADEF$  is cyclic. Now, the key claim is that  $\triangle FBD\sim \triangle ECA$ , which finishes since  $AEC$  is clearly isosceles ( $EB\perp AC$  and  $AB=CB$ ). However, note that
\[\angle{DFB}=\pi-\angle{DFA}=\pi-\angle{DEA}=\angle{AEC}\]
and
\[\angle{FBD}=\angle{ABD}=\angle{ACD}=\angle{ECA},\]
so we're done.

Note that  $\triangle ABC$  is isosceles; thus,  $BE$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle ABC$ . Additionally,  $\overline{AB} = \overline{CB}$  and  $\angle EBA = \angle EBC$  imply  $\triangle AEB \cong \triangle CEB$ . Next, noting that  $EF \parallel BC$ , 
\begin{align*}
\angle FBE = \angle ABE = \angle CBE = \angle FEB,
\end{align*}
so  $\overline{FB} = \overline{FE}$ . Then, noting that quadrilateral  $ABCD$  is cyclic,
\begin{align*} 
\angle AFE = \angle ABC = 180 - \angle ADC = 180 - \angle ADE,
\end{align*}
so quadrilateral  $ADEF$  is cyclic. Now,
\begin{align*}
\angle EDF = \angle EAF = \angle EAB = \angle ECB = \angle DEF,
\end{align*}
so  $\overline{FB} = \overline{FE} = \overline{FD}$ , as required.

