We show that the minimal value of  $[OO_1O_2]$  occurs if and only if  $XC\perp AB$ . The key claim is the following;

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim:**</span>  $\triangle{O_2O_1O}\sim\triangle{ABC}$ .

<span style="color:#00f">*Proof:*</span> Observe that by condition,  $OO_1\perp AX$  and  $O_1O_2\perp BX$ , giving  $\measuredangle OO_1O_2=\measuredangle AXD=\measuredangle AXC=\measuredangle ABC$ ; analogously,  $\measuredangle O_2OO_1=\measuredangle BAC$  and  $\measuredangle O_1O_2O=\measuredangle CAB$ .  $\blacksquare$ It therefore suffices to minimize  $O_1O_2$ . Setting  $M_1, M_2$  to be the midpoints of  $AD$  and  $BD$ , respectively, we have  $O_1O_2\ge M_1M_2$ , since  $O_1M_1\parallel O_2M_2$  and the distance between these lines is  $M_1M_2$ . 

Equality holding is equivalent to  $O_1O_2\parallel AB$ ; since  $O_1O_2\perp XC$ , we equivalently need  $XC\perp AB$ , as desired.  $\square$

Notice that   $$ [OO_1O_2]=\frac{O_1\cdot O_2\sin\angle O_1OO_2}{2} $$  Let  $M,N$  be the midpoint of  $AX,BX$  respectively. Then since  $\angle OMX=\angle ONX=90^{\circ}$ ,  $$ \angle O_1OO_2=\angle MON=180^{\circ}-\angle MXN=180^{\circ}-\angle AXB=\angle ACB $$  which is fixed. Hence in vew of  $(1)$  it suffices to minimize  $O_1\cdot O_2$ .Now let  $R$  and  $R_1$  be the circumradius of  $\triangle ABC$  and  $\triangle ADX$  respectively, then   $$ OO_1=OM+MO_1=R\cos\angle ABX+R_1\cos\angle BDX $$  Since  $\frac{R}{R_1}=\frac{AX}{\sin\angle ACX}\frac{\sin\angle ADX}{AX}=\frac{AC}{AD}=\frac{BX}{DX}$ \begin{align*}
OO_1&=R\left(\cos\angle ABX+\frac{DX}{BX}\cos\angle BDX\right)
&=R\left (\cos\angle ABX+\frac{\sin\angle DBX}{\sin\angle BDX}\cos\angle BDX\right)
&=\frac{R}{\sin\angle BDX}\left(\cos\angle ABX\sin\angle BDX+\sin\angle ABX\cos\angle BDX\right)
&=\frac{R\sin(\angle BDX+\angle ABX)}{\sin\angle BDX}
&=\frac{R\sin\angle CXB}{\sin\angle BDX}
&=\frac{R\sin A}{\sin\angle BDX}
\end{align*}
By symmetry   $$ OO_2=\frac{R\sin B}{\sin\angle ADX} $$  Therefore   $$ OO_1\cdot OO_2=\frac{R^2\sin A\sin B}{\sin^2\angle BDX} $$  which is minimized when  $\angle BDX=90^{\circ}$ < i.e.  $CX\perp AB$ .

