<details><summary>long complex bash solution</summary>We use complex coordinates. Let  $\omega$  be the unit circle, then  $a,b,c$  are fixed and  $x$  varies. Moreover  $\overline{a}=\frac{1}{a}, \overline{b}=\frac{1}{b}, \overline{c}=\frac{1}{c},\overline{x}=\frac{1}{x}$ .

We first compute  $D=\overline{AB}\cap \overline{CX}$ .
\begin{align*}
    d&=\frac{(\overline{a}b-a\overline{b})(c-x)-(\overline{c}x-c\overline{x})(a-b)}{(\overline{a}-\overline{b})(c-x)-(\overline{c}-\overline{x})(a-b)} 
    &=\frac{(\frac{b^2-a^2}{ab})(c-x)-(\frac{x^2-c^2}{cx})(a-b)}{(\frac{b-a}{ab})(c-x)-(\frac{x-c}{cx})(a-b)} 
    &=\frac{cx(b^2-a^2)(c-x)-ab(x^2-c^2)(a-b)}{cx(b-a)(c-x)-ab(x-c)(a-b)} 
    &= \frac{ab(c+x)-cx(a+b)}{ab-cx}
\end{align*}
Moreover, 
\begin{align*}
    \overline{d}&=\frac{\frac{1}{ab}(\frac{1}{c}+\frac{1}{x})-\frac{1}{cx}(\frac{1}{a}+\frac{1}{b})}{\frac{1}{ab}-\frac{1}{cx}} 
&=\frac{(x+c)-(b+a)}{cx-ab} = \frac{a+b-c-x}{ab-cx} \, ,
\end{align*}
hence   $$ d\overline{d}=\frac{(ab(c+x)-cx(a+b))(a+b-c-x)}{(ab-cx)^2}=\frac{(abc+abx-acx-bcx)(a+b-c-x)}{(ab-cx)^2} \, . $$  Now note that  $o_1$  and  $o_2$  can be found by 
\begin{align*}
    o_1&=\left|\begin{matrix}
        x &x\overline{x} & 1 
a &a\overline{a} & 1 
d &d\overline{d} &1 
    \end{matrix}\right|:\left|\begin{matrix}
        x & \overline{x} &1 
a & \overline{a} & 1 
d & \overline{d} & 1
    \end{matrix}\right|=\left|\begin{matrix}
        x & 1 & 1 
        a & 1 & 1 
        d & d\overline{d} & 1
    \end{matrix}\right|:\left|\begin{matrix}
        x & \frac{1}{x} &1 
a & \frac{1}{a} & 1 
d & \overline{d} & 1
    \end{matrix}\right| 
\end{align*}
We compute
\begin{align*}
 \left|\begin{matrix}
        x & 1 & 1 
        a & 1 & 1 
        d & d\overline{d} & 1
    \end{matrix}\right|&= (a-d)+(d-x)+d\overline{d}(x-a)=(x-a)(d\overline{d}-1) 
    &= \frac{(x-a)[(abc+abx-acx-bcx)(a+b-c-x)-(ab-cx)^2]}{(ab-cx)^2} 
\end{align*}
and we compute
\begin{align*}
    \left|\begin{matrix}
        x & \frac{1}{x} &1 
a & \frac{1}{a} & 1 
d & \overline{d} & 1
    \end{matrix}\right| &= \frac{1}{x}(a-d)+\frac{1}{a}(d-x)+\overline{d}(x-a)
&=  \frac{1}{x}(a-d)+\frac{1}{a}(d-x)+\frac{(a+b-c-x)(x-a)}{ab-cx} 
&= \frac{a(a-d)(ab-cx)+x(d-x)(ab-cx)+ax(a+b-c-x)(x-a)}{ax(ab-cx)} 
&=\frac{(x-a)[(ab-cx)(d-x-a)+ax(a+b-c-x)]}{ax(ab-cx)}
\end{align*}
Then 
\begin{align*}
    o_1&=\frac{\frac{(x-a)[(abc+abx-acx-bcx)(a+b-c-x)-(ab-cx)^2]}{(ab-cx)^2}}{\frac{(x-a)[(ab-cx)(d-x-a)+ax(a+b-c-x)]}{ax(ab-cx)}} 
    &=\frac{ax[(abc+abx-acx-bcx)(a+b-c-x)-(ab-cx)^2]}{(ab-cx)[ (ab-cx)(d-x-a)+ax(a+b-c-x)]} 
\end{align*}
We first compute 
\begin{align*}
    N:&= ax[(abc+abx-acx-bcx)(a+b-c-x)-(ab-cx)^2] 
    &=ax[a^2bc+a^2bx-a^2cx+ab^2c+ab^2x-b^2cx-abc^2+ac^2x+bc^2x-abx^2+acx^2+bcx^2-a^2b^2-c^2x^2-2abcx] 
    &=ax(x-a)(a-c)(b-x)(b-c) \, .
\end{align*}
Similarly, we compute
\begin{align*}
    D:&= (ab-cx)[(ab-cx)(d-x-a)+ax(a+b-c-x)] 
    &=(ab-cx)^2\left(\frac{ab(c+x)-cx(a+b)-(x+a)(ab-cx)}{ab-cx}\right)+ax(ab-cx)(a+b-c-x) 
    &= (ab-cx)[abc-a^2b-bc+cx^2+a^2x+abx-acx-ax^2] 
    &=(ab-cx)(x-a)(a-c)(b-x)
\end{align*}
Therefore   $$ o_1=\frac{N}{D}=\frac{ax(b-c)}{ab-cx} $$  and by symmetry   $$ o_2 = \frac{bx(a-c)}{ab-cx} \, . $$  Then  $\overline{o_1}=\frac{c-b}{cx-ab}$  and  $\overline{o_2}=\frac{c-a}{cx-ab}$ .
Now we use the complex shoelace formula in order to obtain the signed area of  $\triangle OO_1O_2$ :
\begin{align*}
    \frac{i}{4}\left|\begin{matrix}
        o_1 & \overline{o_1} & 1 
        o_2 & \overline{o_2} & 1 
        0 & 0 & 1 
    \end{matrix}\right| &= \frac{i}{4}(o_1\overline{o_2}-o_2\overline{o_1}) 
    &=\frac{i}{4}\left(\frac{ax(b-c)(a-c)}{(ab-cx)^2}-\frac{bx(a-c)(b-c)}{(ab-cx)^2}\right) 
    &= \frac{ix(b-c)(a-c)(a-b)}{4(ab-cx)^2}
\end{align*}
Taking the absolute value, we obtain  $\frac{|(b-c)(a-c)(a-b)|}{|4(ab-cx)^2|}$  and since only  $|(ab-cx)^2|$  is not constant, we want to maximize  $|ab-cx|$  in order to minimize the area of  $\triangle OO_1O_2$ .
The minimal value of  $|ab-cx|$  is obtain when  $x=\frac{ab}{c}, \left|\frac{ab}{c}\right|=1$ , which lies on the major arc  $AB$  of  $\omega$ . Thus we see that  $|ab-cx|$  is maximized, when the distance to  $\frac{ab}{c}$  is maximized, which we can do by taking its antipode, i.e.  $x=-\frac{ab}{c}$ . However, note that this  $x$  satisfies  $\frac{a-b}{c-x}+\overline{\left(\frac{a-b}{c-x}\right)}=0$  which is the perpendicularity criterion.
Therefore, the area of  $\triangle OO_1O_2$  is minimized if  $CX\perp AB$ .
This concludes the problem.  $\square$</details>

