Let the radius of \(\omega\) be \(r\). Since \(Y\) lies on the radical axis of \(\omega_1\), \(\omega_2\), we know \[YC^2-YD^2=961^2-625^2.\] Moreover, from \(\overline{EX}\perp\overline{CD}\), we have \begin{align*}     XC^2-XD^2&=EC^2-ED^2     &=(961+r)^2-(625+r)^2     &=961^2-625^2+2(961-625)r \end{align*}

The condition that \(\widehat{PQ}=120^\circ\) implies that \(E\) is a distance of \(r/2\) away from \(\ell\), i.e.\ that \(XY=r/2\); hence, \begin{align*}     XC^2-XD^2&=(YC+r/2)^2-(YD-r/2)^2     &=YC^2-YD^2+(YC+YD)r.     &=YC^2+YD^2+CD\cdot r. \end{align*} But we know \(XC^2-XD^2=YC^2-YD^2+2(961-625)r\), so \[CD=2(961-625)=672.\]

Let  $O_i$  and  $r_i$  be the center and radius  $\omega_i$ , and let  $O$  and  $r$  be the center and radius of  $\omega$ .

