Work with respect to the circles of radii  $961$  and  $625$ . If  $x$  is the distance between the centers, then moving a distance of  $\tfrac r2$  away from the radical axis (where power difference is zero) increases the difference of powers by  $2\cdot x\cdot \tfrac r2 = xr$ . The difference in powers at the center of  $\omega$  is also  $(r + 2\cdot961)r - (r + 2\cdot625)r = xr \implies x = 672$ .</details>

<details><summary>Elaboration</summary>The claim that moving a distance  $d$  from the radical axis increases power difference  $2xd$  can be proved as follows. Let the distance from the centers to the radical axis be  $a$  and  $b$ . WLOG  $a>b$ , and assume that they lie on the same side of the radax (the other case is similar). Then the powers increase by  $(a+d)^2-a^2$  and  $(b+d)^2-b^2$ , and the difference in these is  $(2ad + d^2) - (2bd + d^2) = 2d(a-b) = 2dx$ The center of  $\omega$  is a distance of  $r/2$  away from the radical axis  $\overline{AB}$  due to the  $120^{\circ}$  angle condition.</details>

