Now, we bash with vectors. Let  $\vec{O}$  be the origin, so  $\vec{X} = \vec{A}+\vec{B}$ . Norm stuff yields
\begin{align}
OP^2 &= \vec{P}\cdot \vec{P} \label{eq:eq1}\tag{i}
PA^2 &= (\vec{P}-\vec{A})\cdot (\vec{P}-\vec{A}) \label{eq:eq1}\tag{ii}
PB^2 &= (\vec{P}-\vec{B})\cdot (\vec{P}-\vec{B}) \label{eq:eq1}\tag{iii}
PX^2 &= \big(\vec{P}-(\vec{A}+\vec{B})\big)\cdot \big(\vec{P}-(\vec{A}+\vec{B})\big), \label{eq:eq1}\tag{iv}
\end{align}
from which we can obtain
\begin{align*}
OA\cdot OB \cdot \cos\theta &= \vec{A}\cdot \vec{B}
&= \frac{\big((\mathrm{iv}) - (\mathrm{i})\big) - \big((\mathrm{iii}) - (\mathrm{i})\big) - \big((\mathrm{iv}) - (\mathrm{i})\big)}{2}
&= \frac{(PX^2 - OP^2) - (PB^2 - OP^2) - (PA^2 - OP^2)}{2}
&= \frac{21}{2}.
\end{align*}
Furthermore, we have  $$ OA\cdot OB \cdot \sin \theta = 2[OAB] = [AOBX] = 60 $$  by the sine area formula, so  $$ \tan\theta = \frac{OA \cdot OB \cdot \sin\theta}{OA \cdot OB \cdot \cos\theta} = \frac{60}{\frac{21}{2}} = \frac{40}{7}. $$  Our desired answer is therefore  $40 + 7 = \boxed{047}$ .

