a + b &= -3
ab + bc + ca &= -4
abc = 20
\end{align*}
Letting  $c = \frac{20}{ab}$  and plugging into the second equation we have \[ab + 20 \left( \frac{a+b}{ab} \right) = -4\] Then subbing in  $a+b = -3$  we solve for  $ab$  to find either   $ab = 6$  or  $ab = -10$ . Checking we find we need  $ab = -10$ . Now we have the system,
\begin{align*}
a + b = -3
ab = -10
\end{align*}
Then subbing in  $a = -\frac{10}{b}$  we find that  $a$  and  $b$  are  $-5$  and  $2$ . Answer extraction gives an answer of  $\frac{141}{4}$  so  $\boxed{145}$ .

In what follows, the notation \(x\mid y\pm z\) means \(x\mid y+z\) or \(x\mid y-z\), and \(x\nmid y\pm z\) means \(x\nmid y+z\) and \(x\nmid y-z\).

We prove the following via induction on \(n\):     Let \(S\) be a set of \(n\) positive integers,     none of which is the sum of two different numbers in \(S\).     There is a permutation of \(S\) such that     each of the middle \(n-2\) integers     divides neither the sum nor difference     of its neighbors. 

Our base case is \(n=2\), for which there is nothing to show.

Now assume the permutation \[a_1,\;a_2,\;a_3,\;\ldots,\;a_n\] obeys the required conditions. For \(A>\max\{a_1,\ldots,a_n\}\) with \(A\ne a_i+a_j\) for all \(i\), \(j\), we will show we can insert \(A\) somewhere in \(a_1\), \(\ldots\), \(a_n\).

First note that for all \(i\), \(j\) we have \(A\nmid a_i+a_j\). This is because \(|a_i-a_j|<A\) and \(a_i+a_j<2A\) always.

Now assume for contradiction \(A\) cannot be inserted anywhere. Then the following assertions all hold:      
- \(a_2\mid a_3\pm A\) (else \(a_1\), \(A\), \(a_2\), \(a_3\), \(\ldots\) works);
- \(a_2\mid a_1\pm A\) or \(a_3\mid a_4\pm A\) (else \(a_1\), \(a_2\), \(A\), \(a_3\), \(\ldots\));
- \(a_3\mid a_2\pm A\) or \(a_4\mid a_5\pm A\) (else \(a_1\), \(a_2\), \(a_3\), \(A\), \(\ldots\));
- $\ldots$
- \(a_{n-2}\mid a_{n-3}\pm A\) or \(a_{n-1}\mid a_n\pm A\) (else \(\ldots\), \(a_{n-2}\), \(A\), \(a_{n-1}\), \(a_n\));
- \(a_{n-1}\mid a_{n-2}\pm A\) (else \(\ldots\), \(a_{n-2}\), \(a_{n-1}\), \(A\), \(a_n\));


However, note for all \(i\) that \(a_i\mid a_{i-1}\pm A\) and \(a_i\mid a_{i+1}\pm A\) cannot simultaneously hold, since otherwise \(a_i\mid a_{i-1}\pm a_{i+1}\), contradiction. It follows that \begin{align*}     a_2\mid a_3\pm A     &\implies a_2\nmid a_1\pm A\implies a_3\mid a_4\pm A     &\implies a_3\nmid a_2\pm A\implies a_4\mid a_5\pm A     &\implies a_4\nmid a_3\pm A\implies a_5\mid a_6\pm A     &\implies\cdots     &\implies a_{n-2}\nmid a_{n-3}\pm A\implies a_{n-1}\mid a_n\pm A     &\implies a_{n-1}\nmid a_{n-2}\pm A, \end{align*} contradiction.

solution_md:
This is an application of the [Kronecker-Weber theorem](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kronecker%E2%80%93Weber_theorem).

The problem is equivalent to the splitting field of  $\sqrt[p]{2021 + \sqrt[q]{a}}$  being abelian. Evidently this means  $p=q=2$ . The Galois group is uniquely determined by the images of  $\sqrt{a}$ ,  $\sqrt{2021 + \sqrt{a}}$ , and  $\sqrt{2021^2 - a}$ ; the group is   $D_8$  unless  $\sqrt{2021^2 - a} \in \mathbb{Q}(\sqrt{a})$ , so either  $2021^2 - a$  is square or  $(m^2 + 1)a = 2021^2$ , the latter of which is impssible. Hence, the answer is  $\{2021^2 - k^2 : 1 \leq k < 2021\}$ .

@below: You actually do need K-W to show that certain  $(p,q,a)$  *are* expressible as a linear combination of roots of unity. But you're right on the second part. I've updated my answer to reflect this.

