Note by induction that the four squares do indeed have cheese in them; for they are part of the corner cells of the  $2^{k-1}$  configs. By induction, if the new closed path on  $n = 2^k$  do indeed form a closed circuit, we can see that the four corners (and the eight semi-corners) have cheese in them, completing the first part of the induction. 

Now, we prove the  $\textbf{first bullet point}$  in the closed circuit's definition: when all cheese are placed, the straightaways won't intersect any cheese (in their interior), and all cheese  $\textbf{do signify}$  a right-turn. 

The first is easy; any straightaway which isn't part of the middle straightaway will behave the same way as in the  $n = 2^{k-1}$  config; and the middle straightaway will have the (two) cheese in its interior removed. The second is also not so hard, as
[list = disc] [*] in the bottom-left  $n = 2^{k-1}$  config, the straightaway will end at a  $RD$  cheese, and in the bottom-right  $n = 2^{k-1}$  config, the straightaway will end at a  $UR$  cheese. [*] Conclusively, we can orient the bottom edge of the middle path to the right, so that the straightaway will continue without any obstruction (see diagram for pictorial proof.) [/list]
So, we are done, since the cheese rule will be consistent.  $\blacksquare$  

