Define a  $\textit{vacant cell}$  to be a cell with no cheese (so it theoretically serves as a junction of straightaways; i.e. the only places where they supposedly may intersect) --- and from those cells, define a  $\textit{horizontal}$ ,  $\textit{vertical}$  or  $\textit{intersection}$  cell if the vacant cell admits a horizontal, vertical, or bi-directional straightaways. 

At this point, one might wonder: can a horizontal vacant cell be  $\textit{doubly horizontal}$ ? If not, this opens up the gate for length analysis: we know that the whole grid cannot be treaded by an  $\textit{unending mouse path}$ . 
Turns out, this is true (but not trivial), and we will prove it below. 
