\node at (1.3,-1.8){ $C_{l+2}$ }; \node at (-0.3,0.3){ $C_{l}$ }; \node at (1.3,0.3){ $C_{l+1}$ }; \node at (0,-3.1) { $C_{l-1}$ };
\draw[fill=green] (0,-1.5) circle (2pt);
\node at (0.9,-3.6) {Fig 5':  $C_l-C_{l+1}$  is Type 2};
\end{tikzpicture}");[/asy] $\color{blue} \boxed{\textbf{Bijection Verification Finishing.}}$  
It remains to prove that in each intersection cell, there are at most two direct shots. Note that for each matched intersection cell, for example with  $C_k - C_{k+1}$  (also WLOG in the right direction), it must be the intersection of (somewhat) consecutive straightaways in the form of 
[list = disc] [*]  $C_{k-2}-C_{k-1}$  and  $C_{k+1}-C_{k+2}$  if the closer cheese is  $C_{k-1}$ , [*] or  $C_{k-1}-C_k$  and  $C_{k+2}-C_{k+3}$  if the closer cheese is  $C_{k+2}$ . [/list]
However, if there exists  $l \ne k-1,k$  so that  $C_l-C_{l+1}$  so that it has the same matched cell with  $C_k-C_{k+1}$ , we will consider the sets of straightaways before/after  $C_l-C_{l+1}$  as before. 

By the process in  $\color{red} \spadesuit$   $\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Proof}}$   $\color{red} \spadesuit$ , we know that the segments that form the intersection cell
[list = disc] [*]  $\{(C_{l-2},C_{l-1}), (C_{l+1},C_{l+2})\}$  and  $\{(C_{k-2},C_{k-1}), (C_{k+1},C_{k+2})\}$  if  $C_l-C_{l+1}$  is of the  $\textbf{First Type}$ , or [*]  $\{(C_{l-1},C_{l}), (C_{l+2},C_{l+3})\}$  and  $\{(C_{k-2},C_{k-1}), (C_{k+1},C_{k+2})\}$  if  $C_l-C_{l+1}$  is of the  $\textbf{Second Type}$  [/list]
are equal. This is a no-brainer ---  $l$  must be either  $k$  or  $k-1$ . 

So, as there exists more than  $\dfrac{2}{5}x$  direct shots and fewer than  $\dfrac{1}{5}x$  vacant cells (fewer intersection cells, at that), we have a contradiction. 

We are done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$ <details><summary>After-Contest Reflection and Solution.</summary>Replace the whole entire Section of  $\color{red} \clubsuit$   $\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Upper Bound}}$   $\color{red}\clubsuit$  and  $\color{red} \spadesuit$   $\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Proof}}$   $\color{red} \spadesuit$  with the following observation: 
\[\begin{tabular}{p{12cm}} $\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Observation.}}$  No 3 consecutive straightaways may be  $\color{red} \textit{direct shots.}$ \end{tabular}  \]
Let the number of cheeses be  $N$  and total cells be  $M$ . Now, it's easily computed that the total path length is at most  $N+2(M-N) = 2M-N$  with  $N-1$  total straightaways. 

Dividing the straightaways into groups of three consecutive ones, we get that each group's total length is  $4$  or more (by the  $\color{red} \boxed{\textbf{Observation}}$ ) --- so we get the total length is at least  $\dfrac{4}{3}$  times  $N-3$ ; the number of segments which  $\textbf{must belong to a group.}$  
Putting it all together,
\[ 2M-N \geq \dfrac{4}{3} (N-3) \Rightarrow 6M-3N \geq 4N-12 \]
so the total number of cheese must not exceed  $\dfrac{6M+12}{7} \sim 878\dfrac{3}{7}$  (phew, still hit the mark, even if it's much scarier!) 

We are done.  $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$   $\blacksquare$</details>

