So, can we be sure of deriving a contradiction --- seeing that we only have  $1024-888=136$   $\textit{vacant}$  (i.e. non-cheese) cells to boot, much less intersection cells to contribute (presumably) for 3-in-a-row cheeses? 

By this point, I realized that I was missing the most important ingredient for this;  $\textit{the Long-Unending Path Claim itself.}$  So, I conjured the Claim and proved it using (actually the double-consideration of) a particular cell; that alone took 10-15 minutes to convince myself that no cell can be a part of two horizontal straightaways.  $\color{green} \rule{25cm}{2pt}$   $\color{red} \text{Section 4: Anti-Construction, Bijection Style.}$ Now, the main drive for me to consider the final strats were to combine established ideas into one cohesive sequence: 

First was to consider the actual single-length-ed straightedges instead of plain cheese was the (already mentioned) anomaly that  $887$  segments (or more) supposedly has a total length of  $1160$  or less; prompting around  $600$  of them to be exactly of single length (as in the start of  $\color{red} \text{Section 3: Double Counting Style.}$ ) 

Another primary drive to consider the segments  $\textit{near}$  to  $C_i-C_{i+1}$ , (i.e.  $(C_{k-1}-C_k), (C_{k+1},C_{k+2})$ ) was the abundace of the  $\textit{specific moves}$  of:
\[\begin{tabular}{p{12cm}}
down, down, left, up, right, right, right, down, left, up, up; which is a (nondisjoint) union (with one common edge) of the two sequences 

(down, down, left, up, right,  $\color{red} \text{right}$ ) and  

( $\color{red} \text{right}$ , right, down, left, up, up)
\end{tabular}  \]
An example in the config of  $n = 2^3$  would be the one starting from the cell (row 3, column 6). 

The idea is newly discovered here, but it somewhat relies on the construction (for inspiration) and rough ideas from  $\color{red} \text{Section 2: Cluster Style}$  where small clustered things will be likely to contradict itself. $\color{green} \rule{25cm}{0.4pt}$  
This led me to consider every edge and prove that every intersection cell will be mapped to two direct shots; but not every intersection cell is the intersection of  $C_a - C_{a+1}$  and  $C_{a+3}-C_{a+4}$  as our ideally mapped one. 

To combat that, I considered the other way, so that every direct shot will have its pair by the extremal principle, and no other direct shot other than its pair will have their intersection cell (which is uniquely identified as an intersection of two segments) shared with other direct shots. 

So the counting falsifies itself and I was done; so I could move on to try  $\#3$  and  $\#5$ . 

