I claim that the answer is only  $\boxed{2}.$ Firstly, note that  $$ a_{n+1} = \frac{(a_n+5)(a_n-4)}{2} $$  for all  $n \ge 1.$ Now, if  $p=5,$  then let  $a_1 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}.$  Then, we have
\begin{align*}
a_2 &= \frac{(a_1+5)(a_1-4)}{2} 
&\equiv \frac{6 \cdot (-3)}{2} 
&\equiv -9 
&\equiv 1 \pmod{5}.
\end{align*}Therefore, we will have  $$ a_1 \equiv a_2 \equiv \cdots \equiv 1 \pmod{5}, $$  meaning that the sequence will not contain a multiple of  $5$  in this case.

Next, suppose that  $p \neq 2$  and  $p \neq 5.$  Then, let  $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{p}.$  Then, we have
\begin{align*}
a_2 &= \frac{(a_1+5)(a_1-4)}{2} 
&\equiv \frac{10 \cdot 1}{2} 
&\equiv 5 \pmod{p}.
\end{align*}Therefore, we will have  $$ a_1 \equiv a_2 \equiv \cdots \equiv 5 \pmod{p}, $$  from which it results that the sequence will not contain a multiple of  $p$  in this case.

Finally, it remains to show that the sequence must contain an even element. Suppose for the sake of contradiction that the sequence indeed does not contain an even element. I will prove the following lemma:

Claim: For all positive integers  $k \ge 2,$  all of the elements of the sequence are odd if and o $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^k}.$ Proof. For the base case, since  $a_2$  must be odd, we have
\begin{align*}
a_2 \equiv 1 \pmod{2} &\implies \frac{(a_1+5)(a_1-4)}{2} \equiv 1 \pmod{2} 
&\implies (a_1+5)(a_1-4) \equiv 2 \pmod{4} 
&\implies a_1 \equiv 1 \pmod{4}.
\end{align*}Now, assume that the desired result holds for  $k=i$  for some positive integer  $i \ge 2,$  i.e. in order for all elements of the sequence to be odd, we must have  $$ a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^i}. $$  Then, if we forget about the first element of the sequence and consider the sequence  $a_2, a_3, \ldots,$  it is evident that the same result must hold for the new first element, i.e.  $$ a_2 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^k}. $$  Since  $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^k},$  we either have  $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^{k+1}}$  or  $a_1 \equiv 2^k+5 \pmod{2^{k+1}}.$  In the latter case, if we let  $a_1=2^{k+1}h+2^k+5,$  then we have
\begin{align*}
a_2 &= \frac{(a_1+5)(a_1-4)}{2} 
&= \frac{(2^{k+1}h+2^k+10)(2^{k+1}h+2^k+1)}{2} 
&= (2^kh+2^{k-1}+5)(2^{k+1}h+2^k+1) 
&\equiv 2^{k-1}+5 
&\not\equiv 5\pmod{2^k},
\end{align*}contradiction. Therefore, we must have  $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^{k+1}},$  concluding the inductive step.

Hence, by the Principle of Mathematical Induction, we have proven the claimed result.  $\blacksquare$ However, since  $a_1 \equiv 5 \pmod{2^k}$  for all  $k \ge 2,$  it follows that  $a_1=5,$  which violates the given condition that  $a_1>5.$  Therefore, our original supposition that all elements of the sequence are odd must have been false, implying that there exists some even element. Hence, we are done.  $\square$

